ica. 1991;88:6333–6337. doi: 10.1073/pnas.88.14.6333. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Weider B, Forshufvud S, Forshufvud S. Assassination at St Helena revisited. John Wiley & Sons; New York: 1995. [ Google Scholar ] Xin H, Stone R. Tainted milk scandal. Chinese probe unmasks high-tech adulteration with melamine. Science. 2008;322:1310–1311. doi: 10.1126/science.322.5906.1310. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Yang VL, Batlle D. Acute renal failure from adulteration of milk with melamine. TheScientificWorldJournal. 2008;8:974–975. doi: 10.1100/tsw.2008.137. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',7,'2025-10-15 09:40:28','2025-10-15 09:40:28');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4175,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10026356','Imaging Challenges in Chronic Dissection.','Shen J, Mastrodicasa D, Tse JR, Fleischmann D.','2022','unrepaired malperfusion tevar pressurization multiphasic reformatted submillimeter tevar pseudoaneurysms reconstructing fifties pulsation pulsations multisequence spoiled precession ferumoxytol superparamagnetic ferumoxytol undiluted ferumoxytol ferumoxytol ferumoxytol endoleaks ferumoxytol ferumoxytol ferumoxytol nitinol endograft perigraft lumens counterbalanced ferumoxytol systole laminar lumens lumens opacify outstrip thoracoabdominal pararenal kilovolt tortuous degrades tevar inhomogeneities dephasing nitinol struts tevar ferumoxytol caging caging artifactually unrepaired unrepaired malperfusion diligent malperfusion pneumatosis iliofemoral malperfusion jejunal hypoenhancement pneumatosis iliofemoral intimomedial balled intimomedial redissection stiffer thicker suprarenal suprarenal periaortic stranding hyperattenuation bloody periaortic stranding bloody hyperattenuating malperfusion pseudoaneurysm reintervention redo sternotomy perigraft gallium indium perigraft perigraft perigraft redo sternotomy explanted perigraft anastomoses endoleaks sine endoleaks endograft endoleaks endoleak multiphasic endoleaks endoleak perigraft tevar endoleak reintervention tevar endoleaks perigraft landing tortuosity angulation kinking antegrade kinked sine sine landing sine pseudoaneurysm tevar sine perigraft sine unrepaired domenico mastrodicasa 5t32eb009035 shareholder segmed segmed justin dominik fleischmann bracco hiratzka bakris aats e266 e369 fleischmann afifi casanegra e000075 erbel aboyans boileau appoo bonci steigner 11s s570 s583 rylski pacini beyersdorf heuts schalla ramaekers mjfg zierler baliyan shaqdan hedgire iezzi cotroneo filippone endoleaks flors leiva salinas aghayev steigner fleischmann molvin sherrah vallely grieve e157 e159 sherrah grieve ferumoxytol w366 w373 kathuria ferumoxytol ersoy endoleak clough giese markl schiebler pruijssen guala dux santoy chaturvedi oppenheimer rajiah laan endovasc merkle endoprostheses kanasaki peterss rakita newatia squirrell orton leveen saigh saremi hassani sine','Abstract Chronic aortic dissection comprises a heterogeneous group of unrepaired and repaired disease requiring lifelong clinical and imaging surveillance. CT and MRI are the main imaging modalities for longitudinal surveillance, with growing interest in emerging imaging techniques for prognostic potential. Imaging difficulties span technical and diagnostic challenges, some of which are unique to the repaired aorta, with specific complications depending on the type of repair. This review describes existing and emerging imaging techniques, outlines the technical and diagnostic challenges encountered at CT and MRI, and highlights the diagnostic pitfalls of chronic aortic dissection. Introduction Aortic dissection involves a tear in the aortic wall with preexisting medial degeneration, allowing blood to flow within a separate channel or false lumen. Dissections that involve the ascending aorta are categorized as type A by the Stanford classification, while those that spare the ascending aorta are designated as type B. This distinction has management implications in the acute setting, with most type A dissections treated with open surgical repair. Complicated features of end-organ malperfusion and rupture with type B dissections at presentation are additional considerations that may necessitate urgent thoracic endovascular aortic repair (TEVAR). 1 – 4 Regardless of the initial treatment, chronic aortic dissection requires long-term imaging surveillance to monitor for late complications. Patients that survive the acute event are a heterogeneous group of uncomplicated type B, complicated type B, and repaired type A dissections with persistent dissection of distal aortic segments. Continued false lumen pressurization and wall stress can lead to late complications of aneurysmal degeneration and rupture, which may require open surgical and/or endovascular repair. 2 Serial imaging of the entire aorta and its branches with a multiphasic protocol is necessary to provide accurate measurement, assess false lumen features, and evaluate integrity of prior repairs. 2 – 7 Understanding the technical and diagnostic challenges of imaging chronic aortic dissection is important to identify complications and ensure timely repair. Existing and Emerging Imaging Techniques Current guidelines recommend multidetector computed tomography (CT) as the modality of choice for serial imaging surveillance, with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) as an acceptable alternative. 3 – 6 , 8 CT and MRI are the focus of this review for their superior spatial and contrast resolution and 3-dimensional (3D) datasets amenable to multiplanar reformatted or double-oblique images for accurate aortic measurement. Computed Tomography CT angiography (CTA) is the preferred modality for chronic aortic dissection imaging surveillance, providing submillimeter isotropic data within a single breath-hold for precise mapping of aortic anatomy. 2 – 6 CT is favored over MRI in the post-TEVAR setting for evaluation of device migration and structural integrity. 3 , 5 Standard CT protocols include unenhanced and angiographic acquisitions, with additional delayed-phase imaging after endovascular repair. Unenhanced images help identify high-attenuation material that could be mistaken for pseudoaneurysms or false lumen entry flow. Arterial-phase images provide the high contrast-to-noise ratio required for evaluating the dissected aorta and its branches to provide a detailed anatomic roadmap ( Fig. 1 ). 9 The length of the delay is usually 60–120 seconds, although some recommend the use of dual-energy CT (DECT) and a longer delay of 300 seconds to detect low-flow within the false lumen. 4 , 5 , 10 , 11 Others propose a single-acquisition DECT protocol using a split-bolus injection technique and reconstructing virtual unenhanced images for radiation dose reduction, 12 although the excess risk of radiation-induced cancer is estimated at 0.43–0.65% for patients in their early fifties and even lower with advancing age. 2 Figure 1. Open in a new tab Importance of phase of contrast for chronic aortic dissection evaluation in a 59-year-old man. (A) Axial CTA shows a dissection flap (arrowheads) in the descending thoracic aorta. (B) The dissection flap (arrowheads) is less apparent on axial contrast-enhanced delayed-phase CT, demonstrating the importance of arterial-phase imaging for anatomic mapping of aortic dissection. CT, computed tomography; CTA, CT angiography. Additional considerations include electrocardiogram-gating for reliable measurement of the aortic root and ascending aorta, the evaluation of which can be degraded by transmitted cardiac pulsation artifacts ( Fig. 2 ). 2 , 3 , 9 , 12 Motion suppression with a high-pitch helical acquisition mode is an alternative. 9 , 13 Figure 2. Open in a new tab Electrocardiogram (ECG)-gating delineates ascending aortic pathology in a 63-year-old man with a repaired type A aortic dissection. (A) Axial CTA image of the native proximal ascending aorta shows a linear filling defect with ill-defined margins (arrow), which can be confused for transmitted cardiac pulsations related to non-ECG-gated technique. (B) Axial CTA image at the same level with ECG gating clarifies that the abnormality is a residual dissection (arrow) rather than artifact. CTA, CT angiography. Magnetic Resonance Imaging MRI is an alternative to multidetector CT for chronic aortic dissection imaging, with advantages of flow quantification and the lack of ionizing radiation. 14 , 15 Multisequence MRI with contrast-enhanced MR angiography (MRA) consisting of 3D spoiled gradient-echo sequences after intravenous administration of a gadolinium-based contrast agent is the preferred technique for aortic imaging. Unenhanced MRA with steady-state free precession imaging allows bright-blood imaging without the use of intravenous contrast material, although imaging can be degraded by off-resonance artifacts. 5 , 12 , 14 , 15 When gadolinium-based contrast agents are contraindicated, imaging can be performed with ferumoxytol, an ultra-small superparamagnetic iron oxide particle. Ferumoxytol was initially developed as a blood pool agent for MRI but is now approved for iron deficiency anemia intravenous therapy in the United States and Europe. Serious adverse events have been associated with bolus injection of undiluted ferumoxytol, leading the Food and Drug Administration to issue a black-box warning about potential hypersensitivity reactions and recommendation for slow infusion of diluted ferumoxytol over 15 minutes. 16 , 17 Due to the slow infusion, imaging is performed in the steady state with venous contamination at MRA. The long intravascular half-life of ferumoxytol allows for very delayed-phase imaging, and improved detection of endoleaks has been reported with ferumoxytol MRA as compared to CTA ( Fig. 3 ). 18 Figure 3. Open in a new tab Evaluation of stent-grafts by CTA and ferumoxytol MRA. (A) Oblique sagittal CTA image shows a repaired type A aortic dissection with a stent-graft in the descending thoracic aortic true lumen (arrow) and retrograde thoracic aortic false lumen entry flow (arrowhead). (B) Corresponding oblique sagittal MRA image with ferumoxytol demonstrates the patent nitinol-frame endograft (arrow) with retrograde false lumen entry flow (arrowhead) and additional proximal perigraft entry flow (*) not seen at CTA. CTA, CT angiography; MRA, magnetic resonance angiography. Three-dimensional time-resolved phase-contrast MRI (4D flow MRI) is an emerging technique that has garnered interest for its potential prognostic role. Studies have shown regional flow alterations in the true and false lumens, with false lumen stroke volume, velocity, helical flow, and retrograde flow correlated with the rate of false lumen expansion ( Fig. 4 ). 19 – 22 4D flow acquisition is time consuming and commonly requires acceleration techniques at the expense of signal-to-noise ratio and lower-fidelity velocity data, although this can be counterbalanced by imaging with ferumoxytol and taking advantage of its long plateau of vascular signal. 16 Figure 4. Open in a new tab Three-dimensional (3D) time-resolved phase-contrast MRI (4D flow MRI) in a 68-year-old male with a chronic type B dissection. Four-dimensional flow MRI with color-coded streamline visualization during mid-systole shows laminar flow in the true lumen (TL), with some flow directed into the false lumen (FL) (arrow) through a primary intimal tear (arrowhead) in the distal arch. There was no significant pressure gradient (6 mmHg) across the true and false lumens. MRI, magnetic resonance imaging. Technical Challenges High contrast-to-noise ratio and spatial resolution are needed to evaluate the dissected aorta and its branches. Aortic enhancement may be compromised by anatomy and technical factors, although there are strategies for mitigation. 9 , 23 An incorrectly placed region of interest (ROI) for automated bolus tracking can be the cause of poor aortic contrast enhancement at CTA ( Fig. 5 ). The true and false lumens of a dissection are not well-delineated on the unenhanced slice selection image, which can lead to inappropriate placement of an ROI in the false lumen and a delayed or absent scan trigger. Potential solutions would be to monitor in a segment of normal aorta or to use a test bolus instead of automated bolus tracking in patients with known aortic dissection. 23 Figure 5. Open in a new tab Poor automated bolus tracking region of interest (ROI) selection resulting in suboptimal true lumen contrast medium enhancement in a 45-year-old man with a repaired type A aortic dissection. (A) Axial CTA monitoring slice shows ROI placement in the false lumen (arrow) of a residual descending thoracic aortic dissection. (B) Subsequent axial CTA image demonstrates poor contrast medium enhancement within the ascending aortic graft (arrowhead) and true lumen (*) of the residual dissection, with contrast material mostly within the false lumen (arrow). Inadvertent ROI placement within the false lumen resulted in a delayed scan trigger. CTA, CT angiography. Large-capacity vessels, such as dissecting aneurysms, require several seconds to opacify. Poor contrast opacification renders vascular mapping and evaluation of false lumen patency status difficult. Suggested solutions include slowing down the acquisition speed, placing the ROI for bolus triggering in the aortic segment with the largest diameter, and/or a delayed-phase acquisition. 9 , 13 , 23 A high-pitch acquisition mode is generally discouraged for imaging large dissecting aneurysms as the scan time may outstrip the arrival of contrast material ( Fig, 6 ). Figure 6. Open in a new tab Slow contrast material transit in an 80-year-old male with a dissecting aneurysm. Coronal CTA of a large thoracoabdominal aortic dissecting aneurysm shows stent-grafts within the abdominal aorta (arrow) and iliac arteries, with dilute contrast material (arrowhead) in the pararenal abdominal aorta at the time of acquisition. Persistent false lumen entry flow (not shown) was challenging to assess due to poor contrast medium enhancement of the complex endovascular repair on CTA images. CTA, CT angiography. Beyond aortic anatomy, other patient factors can compromise image quality. For example, poor signal-to-noise ratio in an obese patient in Figure 7 limits evaluation of the aortic stent-graft. Although DECT scanners have a larger gantry size and table weight capacity, large body habitus remains a challenge for both conventional CT and DECT due to detector geometry with a dual-source system and tube current limitations with rapid kilovolt switching configuration. 9 Figure 7. Open in a new tab Poor image quality in an obese 39-year-old man with a chronic aortic dissection post endovascular repair. Axial CTA shows a stent-graft (arrow) within the tortuous upper abdominal aorta. Poor contrast-to-noise ratio and signal-to-noise ratio limit evaluation for stent-graft patency and false lumen entry flow. CTA, CT angiography. Patient motion is another factor that degrades image quality, particularly of small branch vessels. High-pitch acquisition mode can suppress motion artifact, although at the cost of spatial resolution and increased image noise. 9 As mentioned previously, the aortic root and ascending aorta are susceptible to cardiac motion artifact, which can be resolved with electrocardiogram gating or high-pitch acquisition mode ( Fig. 2 ). Post-TEVAR MRI is limited for evaluation of stent patency and device integrity. 3 , 5 , 18 The stent frame material is an important consideration, with significant susceptibility artifact associated with stainless steel precluding assessment of the stent lumen due to local field inhomogeneities, spin dephasing, and signal loss ( Fig. 8 ). Nitinol stent-grafts are amenable to MRI, although radiofrequency shielding artifact reduces the luminal signal of small branch vessels and limits assessment for subtle in-stent thrombosis ( Fig. 9 ). 24 , 25 Some recommend supplemental chest radiograph or unenhanced CT to assess device integrity since the stent struts cannot be visualized by MRI, although CTA remains the modality of choice for post-TEVAR imaging. 3 , 5 Figure 8. Open in a new tab Limited evaluation of a stainless-steel stent in a 59-year-old man with a chronic type B aortic dissection treated with endovascular repair. (A) Significant susceptibility artifact precludes luminal assessment of a stainless-steel stent in the left renal artery on coronal MRA (arrowhead). Both the abdominal aorta (arrow) and inferior vena cava (*) are filled with contrast material owing to the blood pool properties of ferumoxytol. CTA, CT angiography; MRA, magnetic resonance angiography. Figure 9. Open in a new tab RF caging effects limit evaluation of branch vessel stents in a 66-year-old man with a chronic aortic dissection. (A) Axial CTA image of the dissected abdominal aorta shows an occluded right renal artery stent (arrow) and a widely patent stent in the superior mesenteric artery (arrowhead). (B) Axial MRA image of the same patient also demonstrates occlusion of the right renal artery (arrow), although the stent is difficult to delineate. RF caging effects cause the superior mesenteric artery stent lumen to appear artifactually narrowed (arrow). CTA, CT angiography; MRA, magnetic resonance angiography; RF, radiofrequency. Diagnostic Challenges Chronic aortic dissection includes unrepaired and repaired aortas with some shared and distinct complications. Both unrepaired and repaired dissections share the potential risk of aneurysmal degeneration, malperfusion, new dissection, and rupture, 2 , 3 while open surgical and endovascular interventions are subject to additional complications related to the repair itself that are important to recognize. Aneurysms develop in 34–38% of chronic type B dissections and up to 49% of type A repairs with a residual dissection of distal aortic segments. Aneurysm formation is the main reason for reoperation in chronic aortic dissection and requires diligent comparison with prior studies and careful measurement as it reaches the size threshold for repair. 2 Malperfusion, typically manifesting as limb or mesenteric ischemia, is a rare late complication occurring in 6% of patients with chronic aortic dissection. 2 Imaging findings of bowel ischemia on CT include bowel wall thickening, absent or diminished bowel wall enhancement, and pneumatosis intestinalis ( Fig. 10 ). 26 If acute mesenteric ischemia is suspected, a delayed-phase scan can be helpful to assess bowel wall enhancement. Limb ischemia is primarily diagnosed on the basis of patient symptoms of claudication since dissections often involve the iliofemoral arteries and hemodynamic significance is difficult to ascertain by imaging alone. Dynamic obstruction of branch vessels can be difficult to differentiate from static obstruction at a single time point. 4 While 4D CT can be helpful, obstruction is ultimately determined in conjunction with clinical factors suggesting ischemia ( Fig. 11 ). Figure 10. Open in a new tab Malperfusion in a 64-year-old man with a chronic type B aortic dissection. (A) Axial CTA of the abdomen shows segmental jejunal hypoenhancement and pneumatosis (arrow) with an associated chronic superior mesenteric artery dissection (arrowhead). (B) The same patient had been diagnosed with chronic right lower extremity ischemia earlier due to symptoms of claudication in conjunction with chronic occlusion of the right external iliac artery, as shown on a 3D volume-rendered CTA image of the abdomen and pelvis (arrow). Iliofemoral artery dissection involvement is common and hemodynamic significance is difficult to assess by imaging alone. 3D, 3-dimensional; CTA, CT angiography. Figure 11. Open in a new tab Dynamic intimomedial intussusception into the left subclavian artery in a 49-year-old male with a chronic type A dissection. Oblique sagittal CTA image shows intussusception of balled up intimomedial tissue into the left subclavian artery ostium (arrow) from a primary intimal tear in the ascending aorta (arrowhead). Intermittent obstruction can be difficult to differentiate from sustained obstruction on static images. CTA, CT angiography. New dissection or redissection is another complication that is most commonly seen in patients with Marfan syndrome. The new dissection can involve a previously unaffected segment or the chronically dissected aortic segment, and the latter situation has urgent management implications due to further thinning of the diseased aortic wall. 2 While the dissection flap becomes stiffer, thicker, and less mobile as it evolves into the chronic phase, 27 flap motion at the time of image acquisition can simulate a third flow channel and be confused for a new dissection ( Fig. 12 ). The ill-defined appearance of the dissection flap helps point to motion artifact. Figure 12. Open in a new tab Mobile dissection flap confused for new dissection in a 62-year-old female. (A) Initial axial CTA shows an intimal flap (arrow) in the suprarenal abdominal aorta. (B) Repeat axial CTA image a week later demonstrates 2 ill-defined linear filling defects (arrowheads) in the suprarenal abdominal aorta that represent motion artifact from the mobile intimal flap. This artifact was confused for a new dissection due to the appearance of a third flow lumen, and the patient underwent urgent endovascular repair. CTA, CT angiography. Surveillance imaging is performed to prevent the emergency complication of late aortic rupture, reported in 3–18% of cases of chronic aortic dissection. 2 CTA is the preferred modality for evaluation of acute rupture, which can manifest as rapid enlargement, periaortic fat stranding, hyperattenuation within the aortic wall, or bloody pleural effusion ( Fig. 13 ). 7 , 28 , 29 Figure 13. Open in a new tab Ruptured residual dissection in a 67-year-old man 4 years after type A repair. (A) Oblique sagittal CTA image of the thoracic aorta shows a dissecting aneurysm of the descending thoracic aorta with periaortic fat stranding (arrow) and an adjacent bloody pleural effusion (*) from rupture of distal aortic segments as a late complication in a patient lost to follow-up after type A repair (arrowhead). (B) Unenhanced axial CT image of the proximal descending thoracic aorta demonstrates hyperattenuating material in the aortic wall from fresh blood. CTA, CT angiography; TL , true lumen; FL , false lumen. Surgical Repair In addition to malperfusion and rupture, late complications of repaired type A dissections include aortic root-related events and infection. Aortic root replacement is performed during initial surgery in only 5–10% of cases, and the native aortic root may develop an aneurysm, pseudoaneurysm, or recurrent dissection requiring reintervention ( Fig. 14 ). 7 Figure 14. Open in a new tab Aortic root aneurysm after prior type A dissection repair in a 52-year-old man. Oblique sagittal CTA image of the thoracic aorta shows an aneurysm of the native aortic root (arrowhead) proximal to the ascending aortic surgical graft (arrow). The patient underwent redo sternotomy for aortic root repair 2 years after initial surgery. CTA, CT angiography. Aortic graft infection is a very rare complication, with a reported incidence of 0.2% per year. 7 The diagnosis can be especially difficult in the subacute postoperative phase, as perigraft fluid collections or soft-tissue attenuation can persist for 3 months following surgery ( Fig. 15 ). 30 Both sterile and infected fluid collections can have rim enhancement on delayed-phase CT images, and adjunctive studies are sometimes needed in ambiguous cases. Nuclear medicine gallium-67 citrate or indium-111 white blood cell scans can aid in the detection of early graft infection. 30 Late anastomotic leaks should also raise suspicion for graft infection. 7 Figure 15. Open in a new tab Sterile and infected perigraft fluid collections in 2 patients. (A) Axial contrast-enhanced CT shows a rim-enhancing perigraft fluid collection (arrow) along a type A repair in a 57-year-old man. The collection decreased in size on follow-up imaging and represented a resolving postoperative hematoma. (B) Axial contrast-enhanced CT demonstrates a similar rim-enhancing perigraft fluid collection (arrow) along a type A repair in a 73-year-old man. (C) Abnormal radiotracer uptake (arrow) is seen on the second patient’s corresponding positron emission tomography (PET)/CT with 2-deoxy-2-[fluorine-18]fluoro-D-glucose (18F-FDG). The patient underwent redo sternotomy with coagulase-negative Staphylococcus cultured from the explanted surgical material. Sterile and infected perigraft fluid collections are difficult to differentiate by CT, especially in the early postoperative period. CT, computed tomography. Endovascular Repair While endovascular repairs do not have surgical anastomoses and therefore cannot develop anastomotic leaks, they are still subject to aneurysm development (usually of untreated segments) and have endovascular-specific complications of endoleaks, device migration, and stent graft-induced new entry tears (SINE). 5 , 31 – 33 Endoleaks are persistent false lumen entry flow despite attempted exclusion by primary intimal tear coverage. A classification system of false lumen entry flow around the endograft has been defined by the Society for Vascular Surgery/Society of Thoracic Surgeons writing committee that mirrors the classification of aneurysm endoleaks. 4 Endoleak evaluation requires multiphasic imaging to assess the dynamic filling of the false lumen, especially in the setting of low-flow endoleaks that may not be apparent on the angiographic phase. 2 – 6 Even with data from 2 time points in the arterial and delayed phases, the source of the endoleak can be difficult to establish on static images ( Fig. 16 ). Figure 16. Open in a new tab Ambiguous false lumen entry flow source in a 64-year-old male with a type B aortic dissection after endovascular repair. Oblique sagittal CTA image of the thoracic aorta demonstrates a stent-graft (arrow) in the proximal and mid descending thoracic aorta with persistent false lumen entry flow (*). The highest attenuation contrast material is in the proximal perigraft region (arrowhead), but distinguishing type Ia from type II entry flow is difficult on a static image. The patient underwent proximal TEVAR extension and endovascular occlusion of the left subclavian artery. CTA, CT angiography. Delayed-phase images are not only important for endoleak assessment, but also for false lumen patency evaluation. False lumen patency is an important predictor of regional growth and reintervention rate and should be evaluated as part of chronic aortic dissection evaluation. 4 , 5 The degree of false lumen thrombosis can be overestimated by angiographic phase alone and is better assessed on delayed imaging ( Fig. 17 ). Figure 17. Open in a new tab Overestimation of false lumen thrombosis with CTA alone in a 48-year-old man with a type B aortic dissection, status post TEVAR. (A) Axial CTA of the descending thoracic aorta shows a stent-graft within the true lumen (arrowhead) and no contrast material within the false lumen (arrow). (B) Axial delayed-phase contrast-enhanced CT demonstrates that the false lumen is actually patent (arrow). CT, computed tomography; CTA, CT angiography. Device migration is another endovascular-specific complication that may lead to type I endoleaks (perigraft entry flow) and treatment failure. Durable endovascular repair requires a landing zone of at least 15 mm of parallel lie within normal aorta. 2 Aortic arch anatomic factors, specifically tortuosity and angulation, have been identified as risk factors for device migration. 31 Changes may be subtle over time and require side-by-side comparison with more remote examinations, preferably on a 3D workstation ( Fig. 18 ). Figure 18. Open in a new tab Progressive stent-graft kinking and aneurysmal degeneration of a chronic aortic dissection in a 67-year-old male. (A) Oblique sagittal CTA image shows ascending aortic and total arch replacement (arrowhead) with antegrade stent-graft placement in the descending thoracic aorta (arrow). (B) On follow-up CTA 6 months later, the stent-graft is kinked in the arch (arrow). CTA, CT angiography. SINE is a new entry tear within an existing dissection flap and is specific to endovascular repair of aortic dissection ( Fig. 19 ). This is a distinct entity from new iatrogenic dissection related to endovascular approach. SINE manifests as false lumen entry flow through a new defect in the intimal flap at the proximal or distal landing zone. Persistent false lumen perfusion through the SINE can lead to false lumen pseudoaneurysm formation requiring TEVAR extension. 32 , 33 Figure 19. Open in a new tab Stent graft-induced new entry tear tear (SINE) in a 60-year-old man. (A) Oblique sagittal CTA of the thoracic aorta shows a type A combined intramural hematoma/dissection. (B) Postoperative oblique sagittal CTA demonstrates a type A repair (arrow) with a stent-graft in the descending thoracic aorta and proximal perigraft entry flow (arrowhead) from SINE. CTA, CT angiography. Conclusion Chronic aortic dissection is a lifelong disease that encompasses a heterogeneous group with morphologic and hemodynamic differences. Late complications can arise in the unrepaired and repaired aorta, which require serial imaging with CT or MRI for surveillance. Many technical and diagnostic pitfalls are encountered at CT and MRI that are important to recognize for appropriate diagnosis and treatment. Financial disclosures: Domenico Mastrodicasa: Research grant from the National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering (5T32EB009035). Shareholder of Segmed, Inc. Consultant for Segmed, Inc. Justin R. Tse: Grant from Bayer HealthCare, LLC. Dominik Fleischmann: Grants or pending grants from GE Medical Systems and Siemens Medical Solutions, and payment for lectures from the Bracco Group. References 1. Hiratzka LF, Bakris GL, Beckman JA, et al. : ACCF/AHA/AATS/ACR/ASA/SCA/SCAI/SIR/STS/SVM guidelines for the diagnosis and management of patients with Thoracic Aortic Disease: a report of the American College of Cardiology Foundation/American Heart Association Task Force on Practice Guidelines, American Association for Thoracic Surgery, American College of Radiology, American Stroke Association, Society of Cardiovascular Anesthesiologists, Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions, Society for Interventional Radiology, Society of Thoracic Surgeons, and Society for Vascular Medicine. Circulation 121: e266–e369, 2010.. 2010 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Googl