ties that would be managed differently in the clinical setting. Those with loss of ER who were excluded from further analysis were less likely to report a SAB PAR (OR 7.3; 7.3, 95%CI 0.9, 57.0) and had a higher prevalence of GHJ arthropathy on x-ray compared to those with full passive ER (17% vs 3% respectively) (OR 7.2, 95%CI 1.6, 33.1; p = 0.025). There were no other differences in demographic characteristics or other imaging features in those who were excluded from analysis due to a loss of passive ER (p>0.05). Table 4. Diagnostic accuracy of clinical examination variables for a positive response to diagnostic injection into the subacromial bursa. (N = 196). Clinical examination variables Sens (95% CI) Spec (95% CI) PPV (95% CI) NPV (95% CI) LR+ (95% CI) LR- (95% CI) DOR (95% CI) Age > 50 yrs 29 (19, 41) 69 (60, 76) 32 (21, 44) 65 (57, 73) 0.91 (0.57, 1.42) 1.04 (0.84, 1.25) 0.9 (0.5, 1.7) Traumatic onset † 26 (17, 37) 56 (48, 64) 23 (15, 34) 60 (51, 68) 0.59 (0.37, 0.90) 1.32 (1.06, 1.63) 0.4 (0.2, 0.9) Strain injury † 55 (43, 66) 65 (57, 73) 44 (34, 55) 74 (65, 81) 1.58 (1.13, 2.17) 0.70 (0.51, 0.91) 2.3 (1.3, 4.1) Lateral shoulder pain † 20 (12, 31) 80 (72, 86) 33 (21, 49) 66 (59, 73) 0.99 (0.54, 1.75) 1.00 (0.85, 1.15) 1.0 (0.5, 2.1) Anterior shoulder pain † 42 (31, 54) 73 (65, 80) 44 (33, 57) 71 (63, 78) 1.58 (1.05, 2.33) 0.79 (0.61, 0.98) 2.0 (1.1, 3.7) Night pain disturbs sleep 58 (46, 69) 51 (42, 59) 37 (28, 47) 71 (61, 79) 1.18 (0.88, 1.53) 0.83 (0.59, 1.14) 1.4 (0.8, 2.6) Painful arc in abduction 56 (43, 67) 39 (30, 48) 35 (26, 44) 60 (48, 71) 0.91 (0.68, 1.17) 1.14 (0.79, 1.63) 0.8 (0.4, 1.5) Resisted abd. or ER pain 76 (64, 85) 18 (12, 25) 33 (26, 40) 58 (42, 72) 0.92 (0.77, 1.06) 1.39 (0.78, 2.42) 0.7 (0.3, 1.4) Resisted int rotation pain 50 (38, 62) 52 (44, 61) 36 (27, 46) 67 (57, 75) 1.05 (0.77, 1.41) 0.96 (0.70, 1.26) 1.1 (0.6, 2.0) Full passive ER (0°) ǂ 99 (92, 100) 11 (7, 17) 36 (30, 43) 93 (70, 99) 1.11 (1.02, 1.20) 0.14 (0.02, 0.79) 7.3 (0.9,