INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4183,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7972382','Telephonic Medical Toxicology Service in a Low-Resource Setting: Setup, Challenges, and Opportunities.','Hitti E, El Zahran T, Hamade H, Morgan BW, Kazzi Z.','2021','shouldered teleconsult teleconsult teleconsult teleconsult teleconferencing uniting getup telephonic aubmc telephonic aubmc telephonic telephonic aubmc telephonic aubmc aubmc telephonic teleconsult teleconsult telephonic privileging aubmc aubmc amion newtown aubmc aubmc publicized teleconsult zoology caller toxtidbits telephonic scorpions zoology botany herbicides pufferfish mushrooms aubmc howland edmonson pancotti goldfrank tx8001803 banagozar delirrad alizadeh bcpt thapa getup screnci armenia bf02260891 skolnik nordt deldar bahaadinbeigy tara maddry wartime skolnik chemical_safety poisons_centres hitti zahran hamade telephonic amazonaws 20coding 20users 20manual 28may','Abstract Poisoning and envenomation are a global health problem for which the mortality burden is shouldered heavily by middle- and low-income countries that often lack poison prevention programs and medical toxicology expertise. Although telehealth or teleconsult services have been used to bridge the expertise gap between countries for multiple specialties, the use of medical toxicology teleconsult services across borders has been limited. We aim to describe the use of a United States-based medical toxicology teleconsult service to support patient care at a hospital in a middle-income country that lacks this expertise. This report outlines the logistics involved in setting up such a service, including the challenges and opportunities that emerged from establishing medical toxicology teleconsult service in a low-resource setting. INTRODUCTION Poisonings and envenomations are an important global health problem. It is estimated that up to 75% or more of all poisoning-related deaths occur in low- and middle-income countries (LMIC). 1 The lack of poisoning prevention programs and the scarcity of medical toxicologists have been recognized as contributing factors to poisoning in LMICs. 2 – 5 Additionally, many of these countries lack the infrastructure for a national poison control center that would play a crucial role in guiding preventive measures. While multiple studies have shown that patients managed by medical toxicologists have reduced hospital length of stay, hospital costs, and mortality compared with other patient groups where no medical toxicologist was involved, 6 – 7 many countries lack medical toxicology training programs and expertise. A number of international collaborations have attempted to bridge medical toxicology education gaps in LMICs. These include teleconferencing networks through the Global Educational Toxicology Uniting Project (GETUP) program, supported by the American College of Medical Toxicology. 4 Alternatively, teleconsultation has also been used in some countries to seek clinical expertise. Teleconsultation is defined as a “synchronous or asynchronous consultation using information and communication technology to omit geographical and functional distance.” 8 It allows physicians in remote or low-resource areas to access specialist opinion that would otherwise be unavailable to them. Teleconsultation has been used in pediatrics, orthopedics, and general medicine to assist in decision-making, treatment plan, and referral. 8 This platform of communication and delivery of care has also been increasingly used in international settings. One such example is the use of a toxicology teleconsultation, through an electronic mail system, by the US military and government healthcare providers serving overseas. 9 While clinical teleconsultation is rising in popularity, the concept is not new to medical toxicologists through their roles at poison centers worldwide. 10 For multiple decades, medical toxicologists have used poison center telephonic services as a means to provide expert input to the public and healthcare providers. In 2019, the World Health Organization registered 331 poison centers worldwide, with centers varying in their capacity and capabilities. 11 However, this valuable resource is not available in all countries. In Lebanon there is limited medical toxicology expertise and no national poison center to support providers with patient care. While Lebanon is classified as an upper-middle-income country, it faces many of the challenges of low-resource settings especially in healthcare: its healthcare system is fragmented with the majority of hospitals in the private sector, concentrated in urban areas, leaving more rural settings with limited access to quality care. Furthermore, the hospital accreditation process is limited to basic standard review leading to large variations in hospital capabilities and quality of service. Finally, 80% of the healthcare budget is spent on acute care primarily delivered in private hospitals, leaving the public sector under-resourced with no established national poison center. In addition, the field of emergency medicine (EM) is newly developed with the first residency training program established in 2012 and, as of yet, there are no local or regional training programs for medical toxicologists. To address the limited access to medical toxicology specialists in Lebanon, the American University of Beirut Medical Center (AUBMC) initiated a telephonic medical toxicology service through a collaboration with the Medical Toxicology Section of the Department of Emergency Medicine at Emory University in Atlanta, GA. The clinical service team included an AUBMC toxicology officer with training in nursing and quality management who performed consultation follow-ups. Additionally, the team included international medical toxicology fellows who are non-US physicians with specialty training in EM, internal medicine, or pediatrics from their home country. These fellows are training in medical toxicology over a 24-month period at Emory University and function as the Certified Specialist in Poison Information within poison centers, handling the initial call and data collection, in addition to providing their toxicology recommendations after discussion with the medical toxicology physician on-call. The telephonic service, established in 2015, has provided prompt clinical toxicology consultation for 669 patients to date. In this report we describe the initial set-up and service development, as well as challenges. DISCUSSION Conception and Telephonic Service Infrastructure In 2015 a memorandum of understanding between AUBMC and Emory University was established whereby Emory medical toxicology faculty in the US would offer round-the-clock telephonic support for toxicology cases at AUBMC ED and medical center. AUBMC is a 358-bed tertiary care medical center, with an ED that receives approximately 55,000 visits and approximately 25,000 inpatient admissions annually. Pediatric patients comprise 20% of the ED visits and 17% of hospital admission. A medical toxicology faculty member at Emory University served as the medical director for the service, overseeing the call schedule and the database quality assurance process. While data entry occurred for quality assurance purposes, no formal consultation note by the medical toxicologists was documented in the chart. This resolved any medical liability issues for the medical toxicologist, while the quality assurance process ensured reliability and accountability of the telephonic advice. Population Health Research Capsule. What do we already know about this issue? Telehealth services have been used to bridge expertise gaps between countries for multiple specialties. What was the research question? We sought to describe the use of a US-based medical toxicology teleconsult service to support patient care at a hospital in a middle-income country. What was the major finding of the study? Our team successfully implemented a telehealth toxicology service; international collaboration is a viable opportunity. How does this improve population health? A medical toxicology teleconsult service can support patient care related to toxicology-related exposure, in a low-resource country. Since this was not a telemedical consultation service but rather a telephonic resource, Joint Commission requirements for credentialing and privileging the medical toxicologists at AUBMC were not required. Communication The utilization of the service was not mandatory and was made at the discretion of the treating physician. On-call toxicologist’s contact information was made available on AUBMC’s online scheduling program (Amion, Newtown, MA). The consultation request was handled by one of the three fellows at the Emory International Medical Toxicology Fellowship Program in Atlanta, GA, who collected data regarding the history, physical exam, and test results. The assessment and plan were finalized with one of the three medical toxicology supervising attendings. Following the implementation of the electronic health record system (EPIC, Verona, WI) at AUBMC in November 2018, a toxicology consultation note with the toxicologist’s recommendations was uploaded into EPIC and signed by the on-site medical toxicologist who was ultimately recruited and assumed a third of the toxicology calls as part of a transition to in-house capacity. Language was not a barrier because the communication occurred in English, which is the default language used in all educational and professional activities at the AUB. On the other hand, occasional communication delays occurred because of the time difference. These were resolved through a back-up call system where attendings served as second call. There is usually a six- or seven-hour time difference given Atlanta’s location in the Eastern Standard Time zone. Utilization of Service A total of 669 toxicological consultations have been received by the toxicology service to date. The majority of consults have been from providers at the AUBMC ED, inpatient services, and outpatient ambulatory clinics (92.7%, 1.5%, and 0.7%, respectively). Since the service was not publicized externally, only a few consults were from outside hospitals (3%) and few calls were from the public (2.1%). There was a 90% increase in the utilization of our service from 2015 (71 consults) to 2016 (135 consults), followed by a 21% increase in 2017 (164), and a slight decrease in 2018 (129 consults). A recent descriptive study of all teleconsult cases received between March 1, 2015–December 31, 2018, which included a total of 477 cases, reported the following main findings 12 : adult women and children less than five years old constituted a large portion of the cases; the majority of patients were found to have no effect or minor effects; only 20% resulted in moderate or major outcomes, and envenomation accounted for 3.8% of all the consults; intentional exposures were slightly more common than unintentional ones; almost half of the patients were treated and discharged from the ED and 34.2 % required admission; 49 patients (10.2%) left without completing care and were lost to follow-up, with the majority (N = 38) expected to have minor effects. The most common pharmaceutical agents involved were sedative/hypnotics/antipsychotics, analgesics, and antidepressants; the most common non-pharmaceutical agents involved were household cleaning substances, pesticides, bites, and envenomations. While benzodiazepine exposure was found to be common, opioid exposures were scarce in our population, which could be related to the strict government regulations on opioid prescribing and dispensing in Lebanon. Collaborations with Other Departments The toxicology service initiated several internal collaborations to raise awareness about its capabilities and enhance its effectiveness. The leadership team held meetings with clinical departments, the clinical laboratory, and the clinical pharmacy department, the school of public health, the environmental core lab, and the zoology department. A policy was also established to properly handle hazardous specimens from the ED to the core lab in concordance with our occupational safety and risk management team. The nursing team was involved in all of the above procedures to ensure proper communication and standardize the care of toxicology patients. Poison Database and Quality Assurance The medical toxicology fellows and the toxicology officer entered the cases into REDCap, a free, secure, web-based application designed to support data capture for research studies that is Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act compliant The data included patient demographics, caller and hospital information, xenobiotic exposures, exposure route, history, physical exam findings, lab results, medical outcome, level of care, antidotes provided, and disposition. For the majority of variables, values were coded using a similar coding system adopted by the American Association of Poison Control Centers and used in its National Poison Data System. 13 All patients were followed up by the toxicology officer, 24–48 hours post discharge, and all follow-up calls were documented in the database. For quality assurance, the database was frequently updated and monitored for data completion. The hospital used the database for quality control with oversight by the medical director. The service issued a monthly toxicology report to track the trends and patterns of exposure among the patient population. Toxicology Education To bridge toxicology education gaps, multiple activities were provided to residents, medical students, and faculty members of various departments. These included monthly online webinars, journal clubs, and case discussions on common ingestions tailored to fit the poisoning patterns relevant to Lebanon. Additionally, seminars and grand rounds were provided to other departments to raise awareness about our service and introduce them to the common local toxicological exposures. Residents and fellows were also involved in preparing periodic, clinical case vignettes (ToxTidbits) that were circulated to all of the hospital’s healthcare physicians. LIMITATIONS and FUTURE OPPORTUNITIES The provision of this telephonic toxicology resource faces several challenges and limitations. The inability to interview the patient directly potentially limits the ability of the consulting medical toxicologist to fully assess the patient. The consulting providers also encountered occasional difficulties with pharmaceutical products, local plants, snakes, and scorpions unique to Lebanon and poorly reported and characterized. Additionally, the consultant had to occasionally adjust the assessment and management recommendations to the locally available diagnostic modalities, laboratory tests, and antidotes. To address some of these limitations, consultants sought assistance from the AUB zoology and botany departments. On the other hand, the service offered benefits and opportunities beyond its initial scope. The spectrum of exposures enriched the clinical experience of consulting physicians who do not frequently encounter in the US-specific exposures to chemicals like pesticides, hydrocarbons, and herbicides. This knowledge translated into several, peer-reviewed publications and case presentations. In addition, the initiative led to collaborations with the Ministry of Health including the development of the national health surveillance system for chemical exposures and multiple awareness campaigns on envenomations and poisonings captured through the service (pufferfish, toxic plants, and mushrooms). Additionally, the service has also received consultations from several other Lebanese hospitals, emphasizing the need for such a resource at a national level. Lastly, this collaboration facilitated capacity building and trained one of the AUBMC EM residency graduates in medical toxicology at Emory University. This residency program was established in 2012 and is accredited by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education International. CONCLUSION Our team was successful at implementing a telehealth toxicology service, and international collaboration is a viable opportunity. Future actions should ensure the sustainability and expansion of this resource nationally through local capacity building. This is best achieved through a partnership and formal collaboration between the Lebanese government, academic institutions, and hospitals. Footnotes Full text available through open access at http://escholarship.org/uc/uciem_westjem Conflicts of Interest : By the West JEM article submission agreement, all authors are required to disclose all affiliations, funding sources and financial or management relationships that could be perceived as potential sources of bias. No author has professional or financial relationships with any companies that are relevant to this study. There are no conflicts of interest or sources of funding to declare. REFERENCES 1. Nelson L, Hoffman R, Howland MA, et al. International perspectives on medical toxicology. In: Edmonson KG, Pancotti R, editors. Goldfrank’s Toxicologic Emergencies. United States: McGraw-Hill Education; 2014. pp. 1736–43. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Flora SJ. Status of toxicological research in India. Chem Res Toxicol. 2008;21:1317–9. doi: 10.1021/tx8001803. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Banagozar-Mohammadi A, Delirrad M, Alizadeh A, et al. Status of clinical toxicology education and ethics in medical care of poisoned patients in the Islamic Republic of Iran and a comparison with other countries. Basic Clin Pharmacol Toxicol. 2020;126(6):475–83. doi: 10.1111/bcpt.13380. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Thapa AS. Is GETUP helpful and relevant in a resource poor setting like Nepal? J Med Toxicol. 2016;12(2):216. doi: 10.1007/s13181-015-0510-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Screnci D, Hirsch E, Levy K, et al. Medical outreach to Armenia by telemedicine linkage. J Med Syst. 1996;20:67–76. doi: 10.1007/BF02260891. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Curry SC, Brooks DE, Skolnik AB, et al. Effect of a medical toxicology admitting service on length of stay, cost, and mortality among inpatients discharged with poisoning-related diagnoses. J Med Toxicol. 2015;11:65–72. doi: 10.1007/s13181-014-0418-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Clark RF, Williams SR, Nordt SP, et al. Resource-use analysis of a medical toxicology consultation service. Ann Emerg Med. 1998;31:705–9. doi: 10.1016/s0196-0644(98)70228-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Deldar K, Bahaadinbeigy K, Tara SM. Teleconsultation and clinical decision making: a systematic review. Acta Inform Med. 2016;24:286–92. doi: 10.5455/aim.2016.24.286-292. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Maddry JK, Sessions D, Heard K, et al. Wartime toxicology: evaluation of a military medical toxicology telemedicine consults service to assist physicians serving overseas and in combat (2005–2012) J Med Toxicol. 2014;10:261–5. doi: 10.1007/s13181-014-0398-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Skolnik A. Telemedicine and toxicology: back to the future? J Med Toxicol. 2013;9:217–9. doi: 10.1007/s13181-013-0313-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. World Health Organization. Chemical safety. 2020. [Accessed May 20, 2020]. Available at: https://www.who.int/gho/phe/chemical_safety/poisons_centres/en/ 12. Hitti E, El Zahran T, Hamade H, et al. Toxicological exposures reported to a telephonic consultation service at a tertiary care hospital in Lebanon. Clin Toxicol (Phila) 2020:1–7. doi: 10.1080/15563650.2019.1709643. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. American Association of Poison Control Centers. NPDS Coding Users’ Manual. 2014. [Accessed May 19, 2020]. Available at: https://prod-knowledge-repository.s3-us-gov-west-1.amazonaws.com/references/NPDS%20Coding%20Users%20Manual%20%28May%202014%29.pdf .',2,'2025-10-15 09:40:56','2025-10-15 09:40:56');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4184,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12006695','Resources Allocated for Undergraduate Education in Oral Radiology in European Dentistry Programmes: A Survey Study.','Ahlqvist J, Levring Jäghagen E, Friedlander-Barenboim S, Clarkson R, Ferreira Da Costa J, Fisekcioglu E, Mitsea A, Szopinski K, Lindh C.','2025','dentomaxillofacial dentomaxillofacial ambition ambition harmonise adee dented harmonise dentomaxillofacial eadmfr dentomaxillofacial dentomaxilllofacial dentomaxillofacial eadmfr dfmr eadmfr harmonise ects plural eadmfr expedient umea ects ects ects ects ects ects ects extraoral scull perio diff hygienist hygienist hygienists manikins qbion classmates extraoral scull cephalograms extraoral kahoot qbion tenfold cecdo truthful anonymisation ambition ambition assimilate ects ects ects ects ects manikins eadmfr ambition ahlqvist lindh levring jaghagen umea umea cowpe plasschaert harzer vinkka puhakka walmsley adee adee shanley claffey nattestad dented molteni dento maxillo drage atkin damian farnell eadmfr eadmfr eadmfr v2024 cammen roorda baart bdj orsini crothers walmsley qbion qbion shelley brundton dento maxillo bruyn koole mattheos koole vandeweghe mattheos bruyn bryman rohlin hirschmann dento maxillo plasschaert manogue lindh ects widstrom norske tannlegeforenings tidende banoczy bryce zahra hanks','ABSTRACT Aims The Education Committee of the European Academy of DentoMaxilloFacial Radiology should ‘ encourage and support high levels of graduate and postgraduate education in dentomaxillofacial radiology ( DMFR ) all over Europe ’. For this reason, the committee aimed to formulate a document describing resources allocated for undergraduate education within oral radiology in Europe today that can serve as a benchmark against which dental schools and/or countries with dentistry programmes can relate their undergraduate education. Materials and Methods A questionnaire addressing resources allocated for learning oral radiology was distributed to colleagues involved in oral radiology education in 117 dental schools in 29 countries in Europe. Results The answers represent approximately 13% of the dental schools in 40% of the countries providing dental education in Europe. There was a large variation regarding qualifications among teachers. There were also large differences in reported time dedicated to oral radiology courses, within some parts the allocated time differed by a factor 16. Discussion An ambition to achieve high standards in oral radiology all over Europe should, based on the results of this survey, start with an effort to attain consensus regarding the role of oral radiology in dental education. The involvement of dentists with DMFR specialist training, would support high levels of undergraduate education. Conclusion The results of this survey include information that educators and authorities can relate to in planning and implementation of new or revised curriculum. The ambition should be to increase teachers'' competence and to work towards more harmonised and extensive courses in oral radiology. Keywords: curriculum, dental, education, radiology, undergraduate 1. Introduction Since education ministers of 29 European countries signed the Bologna declaration in 1999, there has been an ongoing effort to converge and harmonise the higher educational systems across the European countries [ 1 ]. The aim has been to make it easier for professionals to move around an integrated Europe and to make it possible for authorities to recognise degrees from different countries. Dental education and degrees are no exceptions, and much work has been done within the Association for Dental Education in Europe (ADEE) [ 2 ] and the DentEd Thematic Network [ 3 ] in order to converge and harmonise the various dental curricula. DMFR is an integral component of diagnosis and treatment planning for clinical dental practice and many aspects of dentistry cannot be performed without using radiographs. Further, dentists have access to several imaging modalities but technological advances pose challenges for the users of these modalities [ 4 ]. A newly qualified dentist should have the range of professional skills required to begin working as part of a dental team where radiography and radiology skills are vital [ 5 ]. Dental education needs to ensure that dental graduates have the competency to be safe independent practitioners that can improve and promote the oral health of the population. Among the objectives of the Education Committee of the European Academy of DentoMaxilloFacial Radiology (EADMFR) are to ‘ encourage high standards in dentomaxillofacial radiology all over Europe ’ and to ‘ encourage and support high levels of graduate and postgraduate education in dentomaxilllofacial radiology all over Europe ’. In addition, the committee should work to ‘ simplify the European directives concerning the education in dentomaxillofacial radiology within the basic training of dentistry ’ [ 6 ]. To fulfil these objectives, it is vital to strive towards all dental graduates in Europe having similar core competencies in DMFR needed to work as professionals in different societal contexts. Furthermore, knowledge gained by the students during their undergraduate education, must rely on a common base among colleagues interested in education to be a registered specialist or gaining a certificate in DMFR in countries where DMFR is not a recognised specialty. To establish a base for the work with learning objectives and teaching/learning strategies the EADMFR education committee decided to investigate similarities and differences regarding allocated resources in undergraduate education within DFMR in Europe today. Surveys have shown that significant variations exist in curriculum structure [ 7 , 8 ], but no survey has focused on DMFR. Therefore, the Education Committee of EADMFR aimed to compile quantifiable information regarding time and resources provided for undergraduate students within the area of oral radiology in European dentistry programmes. The resulting document is intended to show extent, medians and spread of the time and resources provided, and serve as a benchmark against which individual schools, and/or countries, can relate their undergraduate education when planning and implementing changes and/or evaluating curricula. In this way the document can serve as a valuable tool that can be used to harmonise oral radiology education between the European countries and dental schools. 2. Material and Methods During 2017 and 2018 the committee designed questions for the survey with the objective to present a simple and adequate questionnaire that would be easy to fill in for most schools, regardless of differences in curriculum structure. The questionnaire addressed three parts within oral radiology: basics of radiation theory, how to take radiographs, and interpretation and diagnostics. In addition, the questionnaire requested information regarding the competence of the teachers responsible for different parts of and/or otherwise involved in the teaching of oral radiology. There were also basic questions about annual intake of students, use of European Credit Transfer System (ECTS), extent of the dentistry programme and extent of the oral radiology course(s). The plural form ‘courses’ was used to include education composed by several minor courses in oral radiology. The intra‐ and inter‐country variation in reported total time allocated for the three main areas (basics of radiation theory, learning how to take radiographs, and interpretation and diagnostics) was analysed. The variation was determined by calculating the coefficient of variation within each area. This measure relates the standard deviation to the mean value (in per cent). Variation within each main area and country will be related to the mean value within that country. Variation between countries will be related to the mean value for all countries. This means that differences between countries will not influence variations within a country. Parallel to the construction of the questionnaire the committee also worked with the challenging task to collect contact information for staff at the dental schools with knowledge of the training in oral radiology and could reply to the questionnaire. Colleagues were approached based on membership in EADMFR complemented by personal knowledge of colleagues who were not members of the academy at the time of the survey. The combination of an expedient questionnaire and a well‐defined target group was believed to increase the chances of receiving correct information and a high response rate. During the second part of 2018 a web‐based questionnaire was created and a server with an assigned e‐mail address at Umeå University, Sweden, was set up for carrying out the web‐based survey. In the beginning of 2019, the survey was piloted among the members of the committee to test the functionality of the survey and to refine the questionnaire. In May 2019 the survey was distributed to colleagues at 117 different dental schools in 29 countries. As a result, close to 80% of the countries in Europe that provide dental education and just over 53% of all European dental schools were invited to participate in the survey. Since the questionnaire was rather extensive, a slight reluctance to respond could be anticipated from the approached schools. To encourage and facilitate a high response rate, the time for completion of the questionnaire was generous and two reminders were sent out with an interval of 4 months, one in July and one in late October. The survey was closed in the beginning of December 2019. 3. Results Despite a challenging questionnaire the respond rate was good. Close to 26% ( n = 30) of the approached schools in 52% ( n = 15) of the countries responded (Table 1 ). This means that approximately 13% of all European dental schools from close to 42% of the countries with dental education have contributed to the result. The participating schools varied in size with the intake of students each year varying between 40 and 160. Eighty per cent of the schools reported that their dentistry programme was 5 years and 20% had a 5.5 or a 6‐year dentistry programme. TABLE 1. Number of responding schools by country. Country Number of responding schools per country Belgium 1 Finland 2 Greece 1 Iceland 1 Latvia 1 Lithuania 2 The Netherlands 2 Norway 3 Poland 3 Portugal 2 Russia 1 Sweden 3 Switzerland 1 Türkiye 3 United Kingdom 4 Total 30 Open in a new tab The total reported scheduled time dedicated to oral radiology course(s), varied between 22 and 240 h with a median of 101 h. Eighteen respondents in 12 countries stated that they used the European Credit Transfer and accumulation System (ECTS) both for the whole dentistry programme and for the oral radiology course(s). The ECTS for the oral radiology course(s) varied between 3 and 18 ECTS, which means that the course(s), measured as ECTS points, constituted between 1% and 5.5% of the whole dentistry programme. A weak correlation was seen between the declared total time for oral radiology in hours and ECTS points (Figure 1 ). FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Total scheduled time for the Oral Radiology course(s) in hours plotted against number of ECTS points for the Oral radiology course(s). Eighteen schools in 12 countries. (Two schools reported the same number of hours and ECTS points, resulting in 17 visible data points representing the 18 schools). The declared time reserved for students to learn basics of radiation theory, to learn how to take radiographs and to learn how to interpret radiographs varied considerably between the schools (Figures 2 , 3 , 4 ). FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Box‐and‐whisker plots demonstrating the time reserved for students to learn various parts of the basics of radiation theory. FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab (a) Box‐and‐whisker plots demonstrating the time reserved for students to learn different radiographic techniques, (b) Box‐and‐whisker plots (min, max) demonstrating the time reserved for students to learn how to perform intraoral radiographic examinations. ‘Extraoral’: (i.e., anterior–posterior and lateral projection of the scull, e.g., for cephalometric assessment). ‘Advanced’: (i.e., CBCT, CT, MR, Ultrasound). FIGURE 4. Open in a new tab Box‐and‐whisker plots demonstrating the time reserved for students to learn how to interpret radiographs. ‘Perio’ refers to periodontal and includes apical and marginal findings. ‘Diff diagnostics’ relates to differential diagnostics and findings regarding inflammation of the jaws, tumours, cysts etc. 3.1. Basics of Radiation Theory All respondents could identify members of staff responsible for the teaching of basics of radiation theory. A DMFR specialist or teacher stating corresponding postgraduate education within DMFR was responsible in 57% of the schools and a medical physicist in 32%. In 29% a general dentist was responsible and in 18% a radiographer/technician was responsible (Table 2 ). TABLE 2. Educational level of teachers responsible for, and/or involved in, the oral radiology education within different parts of radiography/radiology. Part of radiography/radiology DMFR specialist or corresponding postgraduate qualification (%) Medical physicist (%) General dentist (%) Radiographer/technician + nurse/assistant or dental hygienist (%) Postgraduate student (%) Basics of radiation theory, responsible 57 32 29 18 + 0 Learning how to take radiographs, responsible theoretical part 52 63 44 + 4 Learning how to take radiographs, involved in practical training 48 75 52 + 22 Interpretation and diagnostics, responsible 71 4 61 21 Interpretation and diagnostics, involved in practical training 75 4 7 25 Open in a new tab Note: Values are expressed in percentage of reporting schools. In total, 96.4% of the respondents declared that they had stated learning outcomes on the basics of radiation theory that the students were expected to have achieved at the end of the course(s). In all schools, the students'' knowledge and competence in the basics of radiation theory was assessed and a written exam was the most common way of assessment (85%) followed by oral exam (36%) and continuous clinical examination (28%). Objective Structured Clinical Examination (OSCE) was used for assessment in 10% of the schools, and other unspecified methods in 10%. The longest stated total time dedicated to radiation theory was approximately 10 times longer than the shortest dedicated time declared. Within basic radiation theory, the time dedicated for radiation protection specifically, differed with a factor 16 between the shortest and longest declared time (Figure 2 ). 3.2. Learning How to Take Radiographs In total, 96.5% of the respondents declared that they could identify a member of staff responsible for teaching the theoretical part of how to take radiographs. In 63% of the responding schools, this was a dentist. In 52% it was a DMFR specialist or teacher stating corresponding post graduate education within DMFR, in 44% a radiographer/technician, and in 4% a nurse, assistant or dental hygienist. The extent to which different professions among the staff were involved in the practical education of taking radiographs was as follows; general dentists (75%), DMFR specialists or teacher stating corresponding post graduate education within DMFR (48%), radiographers/technicians (52%), and nurses/assistants/dental hygienists (22%), (Table 2 ). Digital radiography was used in 86% of the schools and both digital and analogue radiography was used in 14%. Altogether, 93% of the respondents had formulated learning outcomes on how to take radiographs that the students were expected to have achieved at the end of the course(s). In 93% of the schools, the student''s knowledge and competence in how to take radiographs was assessed. Various forms for assessment were used, often in combination; written exam (69%) practical competence testing (65%), continuous clinical assessment (54%), oral examination (35%) and OSCE (27%). Pre‐clinical training in taking radiographs was included in 86% of the schools. The training was carried out with manikins (88%), radiographic simulators for example, Qbion Oral radiology package [ 9 ] (13%) and other methods for example, training on classmates without radiation (8%). Sixty‐one per cent of the schools had specified requirements that should be met by the student regarding pre‐clinically performed radiographic examinations with different radiographic techniques. Of these 61%, 82% had requirements regarding partial dental examinations, 71% regarding full mouth series and 65% regarding panoramic examinations. Requirements regarding both extraoral plane radiographs (anterior–posterior, lateral scull examination, cephalograms etc) and other training (simulator training etc.) were formulated by 6% of the course providers with specified pre‐clinical requirements. In 75% of the schools the education included patient examinations. Of these, 76% had requirements regarding partial dental examinations, 71% panoramic examinations and 65% regarding full mouth series. Extraoral plane radiographs were required by 6% and advanced examinations for example, cone beam computed tomography (CBCT) by 6%. The longest declared total time dedicated to practising how to perform radiographic examinations was 8 times longer than the shortest dedicated time. Within the subject ‘learning radiographic techniques’, the time reserved for learning how to perform intraoral examinations differed with a factor 22 (Figure 3a ). The largest differences were found in pre‐clinical training (min 0 h, max 30 h) and examinations on patients (min 0 h, max 36 h) (Figure 3b ). 3.3. Interpretation and Diagnostics In 71% of the schools the member of staff responsible for teaching radiographic interpretation and diagnostics was a DMFR specialist or a teacher stating corresponding post graduate education within DMFR. In 61% it was a general dentist and in 21% a postgraduate student. In 4% a medical physicist was responsible. Ninety‐three per cent of the respondents had formulated learning outcomes on radiographic interpretation and diagnostics that the students were expected to have achieved at the end of course(s). The extent to which different professions among the staff was involved in the practical education of interpretation and diagnostics was as follows; general dentists (75%), DMFR specialists (75%), postgraduate students (25%) and medical physicists (4%) (Table 2 ). Computer‐based learning methods was used in 57% of the schools. Among these, 100% used Power point‐based methods, 75% used interactive computerised learning tools for example, board games (Go‐Fish, Kahoot and Qbion oral radiology package [ 9 ]) and 44% used video lectures. In 93% of the schools, the students'' knowledge and competence in radiographic interpretation and diagnostics was assessed through a combination of tests. Written exams were used in 100%, practical competence testing in 69%, oral examination in 65% and OSCE in 27%. There was an almost tenfold difference in total time dedicated to learning how to interpret radiographs between the highest and lowest reported number of hours. Within specific subjects that are not only parts of the subject radiology even larger differences were seen. Time dedicated to anatomy differed with a factor of 24 between the shortest and longest. Corresponding factor for caries and periodontal diagnosing was 30, and time dedicated to interpretation of images depicting inflammation of the jaws, tumours, cysts, etc. varied between 3 h and 42 h (Figure 4 ). The reported time allocated for the three main areas of education in oral radiology (basics of radiation theory, learning how to take radiographs and interpretation and diagnostics) showed a significantly larger variation between countries compared to within countries. The coefficient of variation regarding basics of radiation theory was within countries as a mean 25.5%. When comparing differences between countries, the value was 42.7%. Corresponding values for learning how to take radiographs was within countries as a mean 24.4% and between countries 62.4%. For learning interpretation and diagnostics, the variation within countries was as a mean 32.9% and between countries 60%. In countries without a recognised specialty, a teacher declaring corresponding post graduate educational level was responsible for the oral radiology education in 50% of the schools. In these schools, the declared total scheduled time for Oral radiology course(s) was 50% higher compared to the 50% of schools where other occupational groups (e.g., dentists, radiographers and dental nurses) were declared responsible. 4. Discussion The survey shows a large variation regarding educational level and competence among teachers in undergraduate oral radiology education in Europe. The largest variation in competence appears among teachers who conduct the theoretical parts of the oral radiology education such as radiation theory and diagnostics, but there is also a variation among teachers involved in the practical parts of the oral radiology education, such as how to perform a radiographic examination. The answers regarding responsibility indicate that there can be shared responsibilities (Table 2 ). The survey also reveals a large difference in declared time dedicated to oral radiography and oral radiology. With regards to radiation protection specifically, the difference could be described by a factor 16. Surveys among dentists based on questionnaires about oral radiology is a widely used research method [ 10 ], however, it is always a challenge to reach the intended survey population. Currently, there is no data confirming the exact number of dental schools across Europe. The most recent Council of European Chief Dental Officers (CECDO) database recorded 220 ‘dental’ schools [ 11 ]. In the current study we made a significant effort to identify schools and contact people with knowledge of oral radiology in undergraduate dental programmes, and in total we managed to reach 117 dental schools in 29 European countries. The response rate regarding approached countries (52%) corresponds to almost 42% of all countries in Europe with dentistry programmes. Analysing data from 15 European countries may be a representative sample and is similar to samples reported in some other survey studies on dental curricula [ 12 , 13 ]. Furthermore, for the results of both schools and countries, the response rates in this survey are considered as acceptable within social sciences regarding survey‐studies such as the present study [ 14 ]. The questionnaire was anonymous, so the responders are likely to be truthful. As it was not possible to identify the non‐responders, their reasons for not completing the questionnaire are unknown. Due to data anonymisation there is no reason to assume that there is a high risk of skewed results. It could be anticipated that schools with more well‐established radiology education would be more prone to answer the questionnaire. However, the relatively large variation in allocated time for the oral radiology courses indicates that the responding schools do not only represent schools with more extensive radiology education. One of the most striking findings of this survey is the difference between schools regarding what proportion of the total dentistry programme is constituted by oral radiology course(s). The shortest reported time dedicated to the radiology course(s) was less than a 1/10th of the longest reported time. An ambition to achieve high standards in oral radiology all over Europe and to encourage and support high levels of pre‐ and postgraduate education in oral radiology should, based on the results of this survey, start with an effort to attain a consensus regarding the role of oral radiology in European dental education. Bearing in mind the importance of and demands on oral radiology in dental care regarding the quality of diagnostics and on justification and documentation, the ambition should be to work towards more extensive courses in oral radiology in the dental education programmes in Europe. The medians shown in the boxplots, represent aggregated data on resources allocated in the different education areas and could serve as minimum standards when revising courses or planning new courses. This recommendation should, however, be adapted to varying pre‐conditions for example, integration in other clinical courses and legal aspects regarding the students'' possibilities of performing radiographic examination and diagnostics of patients outside the oral radiology course. When analysing the result, one should remember that this survey does not include any information regarding the outcome of the education. Furthermore, education in oral radiology can be implemented and integrated in many ways within a curriculum. At the end of the questionnaire, the respondents had the opportunity to add comments if there was anything else they wanted to share regarding their radiology course(s). This opportunity was used by 13 respondents and 7 of these stated that oral radiology in one, two, or all three specified areas was integrated in other courses, mainly other clinical courses. This must be seen as positive as it supports continuous learning within oral radiology throughout the undergraduate curriculum in close collaboration with colleagues from relevant clinical disciplines [ 15 ]. It is of course difficult to specify the amount of time that the students spend gaining knowledge of oral radiology within other clinical courses. However, one can assume that the students, as a result of integration, assimilate knowledge within oral radiology in addition to what can be expected from the time declared in this questionnaire. The part of radiology with the largest differences in reported scheduled time for the students was learning how to take radiographs with different radiographic modalities. However, it is difficult to estimate the total resource allocated for this since, based on the answers in the questionnaire, training in performing radiographic examinations can largely be carried out within clinical courses other than oral radiology. ECTS was initiated for credit transfer and is based on the convention that 60 credits measure the workload of a full‐time student during one academic year. The student workload in a full‐time study programme in Europe usually amounts to 36/40 weeks per year and in those cases one credit equals 24–28 working hours [ 16 ]. Student workload in the ECTS includes the time spent attending lectures, seminars, practical and clinical work, independent studies and preparation for and taking of examinations. The weak correlation between ECTS and allocated time for education in oral radiology in this survey is therefore somewhat surprising. One explanation might be that these values are just a guide, and different schools might include different parts of education within the hours and the exact number of hours included in one ECTS point can differ from country to country. Another explanation for differences in the given ECTS points could be that the educators might have different expectations regarding the amount of time the students spend on independent studies. An interesting result was the differences that probably have a limiting effect on the learning process during the studies. As an example, differences in regulations can for example, limit the possibilities for students to perform radiographic examinations on patients before they receive their licence, which impedes the learning development of this specific skill during the dental education. In such situations, the knowledge and experiences that clinical training on patients results in, can to some extent be gained by preclinical training on manikins, fellow students (without radiation) and/or interactive computerised learning tools. The experience and knowledge gained from examinations of real patients in an environment with experienced teachers will, in these cases, be achieved after graduation and the speed of this development will be dependent on the environment in which the former students start to practice their profession. Although many respondents stated that a DMFR specialist was responsible for, and/or involved in, the oral radiology education (Table 2 ), DMFR is a recognised specialty with national board exams in few European countries (Finland, Norway, Sweden, UK and Türkiye). However, in some European countries, dentists undergo university postgraduate courses of 3 years [ 17 ], and these dentists might have knowledge and competence comparable to a recognised DMFR specialist. When answering the survey, the instructions stated that the category ‘DMFR specialist’ also included teachers with corresponding post graduate education. In the five countries with a recognised DMFR specialty with national board exams, more than one school per country answered the survey and the response rate was higher than in countries without a recognised specialty. As a result, a relatively large proportion of specialists, as defined in the survey, are declared responsible for teaching oral radiology, a proportion that exceeds the proportion of DMFR specialists with national board exams in Europe. We found that 50% more time was allocated for oral radiology education in schools where a teacher declaring corresponding educational level was responsible in countries without recognised specialty. This indicates that there is a correlation between the time spent on oral radiology education and the expertise level of the teachers, which supports the notion that all schools and countries should strive for increased competence in DMFR among the teachers specifically and among dentists in general. The involvement of dentists in DMFR specialist training, or the equivalent, might encourage and support high quality under‐ and postgraduate education. It is difficult to know to what extent this survey can be generalised. However, the results clearly show a significantly larger variation between countries compared to variations between schools within a country, and we draw the conclusion that the reported large variation in allocated resources, do reflect differences between countries. Although a larger amount of allocated resources can be interpreted as the students receiving a more comprehensive and superior education, especially if the teachers possess a higher competence in the area, it is also important to study the learning outcomes that the students are expected to reach and how these are assessed. A large body of work within European undergraduate dental education has aimed at developing learning outcomes within various fields, focusing on harmonisation of dental undergraduate education [ 1 , 18 ]. More than 90% of the respondents in this survey stated that they have formulated learning outcomes within the three areas of oral radiology addressed. In a recent systematic review [ 19 ] dealing with the progress and challenges in the harmonisation of European undergraduate dental education, dental education as whole was studied as well as specific subjects within the curriculum. However, no report on oral radiology was found. To further support the objectives of the EADMFR education committee, a subsequent step from this survey could be to formulate general expected learning outcomes for oral radiology education and propose alignments within Europe. It would also be beneficial to study how learning outcomes are assessed and whether the assessments actually measure the formulated expected learning outcomes. 5. Conclusion The results from this survey study can be useful for educators and authorities when evaluating their course(s) and/or when planning for a new or revised curriculum in oral radiology. The document can serve as a benchmark and valuable tool when working towards a common standard of undergraduate education, and for uniformity between countries and schools with dentistry programmes in Europe. The ambition should be to increase teachers'' competence in DMFR and work towards more harmonised and extensive courses in oral radiology. This is extremely important as the use of ionising radiation in medical as well as dental imaging is constantly increasing and has implications for radiation protection of medical staff, public and patients. Author Contributions All authors contributed to conception, design, collection of contact information and critical revision of the final version of the manuscript. Jan Ahlqvist, Christina Lindh and Eva Levring Jäghagen analysed data and drafted the manuscript. Ethics Statement Participation in the survey was voluntary. By answering the survey, the responders gave their informed consent to participate. The results are based on anonymized answers. No ethical approval for the survey was required. Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Acknowledgements The authors wish to thank research engineer Magnus Johansson, Umeå university, Sweden, for administering the online survey. Funding: The study had no allocated funding. Umeå University supported the study with resources for secured distribution of the survey and collection and storage of data. Data Availability Statement The data is not available. When answering the survey, the informant gave their informed consent to participate under the condition that they were anonymized. References 1. Cowpe J., Plasschaert A., Harzer W., Vinkka‐Puhakka H., and Walmsley A. D., “Profile and Competences for the Graduating European Dentist–Update 2009,” European Journal of Dental Education 14 (2010): 193–202. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. ADEE , “Association for Dental Education in Europe,” (2025), https://adee.org/graduating‐european‐dentist . 3. Shanley D. B., Dowling P. A., Claffey N., and Nattestad A., “European Convergence Towards Higher Standards in Dental Education: The DentEd Thematic Network Project,” Medical Education 36 (2002): 186–192. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Molteni R., “DMFR 50th Anniversary: Review Article the Way We Were (And How We Got Here): Fifty Years of Technology Changes in Dental and Maxillofacial Radiology,” Dento Maxillo Facial Radiology 50 (2020): 20200133. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Drage N. A., Atkin P. A., Damian J. J., and Farnell J., “Dental and Maxillofacial Radiology: Confidence, Knowledge and Skills in the Newly Graduated Dentist,” British Dental Journal 228 (2020): 546–550. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. “The Constitution of EADMFR, Article 4; Objectives 1, 4 and 5,” (2025), https://eadmfr.eu/wp‐content/uploads/eadmfr‐constitution‐v2024‐10.pdf . 7. Brand H. S., van der Cammen C. C. J., Roorda S. M. E., and Baart J. A., “Tooth Extraction Education at Dental Schools Across Europe,” BDJ Open 1 (2015): 15002. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Field J., Stone S., Orsini C., Vital S., Crothers A., and Walmsley D., “Curriculum Content and Assessment of Pre‐Clinical Dental Skills: A Survey of Undergraduate Dental Education in Europe,” European Journal of Dental Education 22 (2018): 122–127. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. “Qbion Virtual Radiology,” (2024), https://www.qbion.com/ . 10. Shelley A. M., Brundton P., and Horner K., “Questionnaire Surveys of Dentists on Radiology,” Dento Maxillo Facial Radiology 41 (2012): 267–275. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Dixon J., Field J., Vital S., et al., “O‐HEALTH‐EDU: A Viewpoint Into the Current State of Oral Health Professional Education in Europe: Part 1: Programme Level Data,” European Journal of Dental Education 28 (2024): 1–16. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. De Bruyn H., Koole S., Mattheos N., and Lang N. P., “A Survey on Underg