INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4186,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11288228','Use of Registries and Large Databases for Toxicology Research.','Mazer-Amirshahi M, Laub DA, Mycyk MB.','2024','nhamcs workings webpoisoncontrol webpoisoncontrol barcodes spis webpoisoncontrol webpoisoncontrol unverified webpoisoncontrol oversees nhamcs nhamcs nhamcs extractors nhamcs nhamcs interstate intrastate nhamcs neds neds nrd samhsa samhsa psychostimulant samhsa sduh samshsa nssats teds samhsa mhss nsduh ssats sumhss teds samsha samhsa realms emit marketscan candid amirshahi webpoisoncontrol gummin mowry beuhler spyker nathaniel ptp bronstein 39th ohrp webpoisoncontrol webpoisoncontrol smolinske litovitz webpoisoncontrol ahcd mccaig geocodes geocodes geowt_nhcs samhsa samhsa samhsa nsduh samhsa ssats samhsa teds faers vaers marketscan 0nkle57y 20ibm 20marketscan 20assessments 20hospital','Abstract The advancement of medical toxicology knowledge has traditionally relied on case reports and case series because of the ethical challenges involved in studying poisoned patients. The growing availability of several large databases and registries now allows researchers to describe and analyze patterns in poisoned patients who share a particular exposure, outcome, or condition. A large database or registry can be useful in generating hypotheses, supporting extramural funding applications, and planning more rigorous studies. Knowing how to access and interpret data in registries such as NPDS, NHAMCS, and HCUP is essential for all stakeholders engaged in medical toxicology research. This review describes the strengths and limitations of different toxicology-relevant registries and databases and how to leverage these powerful tools to advance the science in the field of medical toxicology. Keywords: Toxicology, Research, Registries, Database Introduction Research in the field of medical toxicology can be challenging because it is difficult and may be unethical to perform gold-standard, randomized, controlled trials in poisoned patients. Some exposures are exceedingly rare; the increased occurrence of poisoning with multiple substances makes the study of individual xenobiotics difficult; clinical history may be limited for many reasons, and laboratory confirmation of exposure is rarely obtained because it is not possible to do so in real time, and the results usually do not impact patient care. As such, toxicology research has historically relied on data obtained from case reports, small case series, and poison centers from the National Poison Data System (NPDS) [ 1 ]. In recent years, there has been increased use of data from other large databases and registries to perform research in most specialties, including medical toxicology. In this review, we describe some of these registries and large databases and discuss how they can be used for medical toxicology research. An Introduction to Large Databases and Registries Large databases and registries have been successfully used by public health and toxicology researchers to study a specific population or exposure, to describe trends in treatment or outcomes, or to characterize healthcare utilization over time. Data may be collected by a variety of methods and by different organizations, including governmental agencies, insurance companies, healthcare systems, and academic organizations. Use of large databases with thousands or millions of exposures will generally yield an adequate sample size, except in the case of very rare exposures. The large number or “ N ” can allow for the evaluation of trends over time, geographic variability, patient demographics, treatments provided, resources used, and medical outcomes. It makes sense that study findings from a multi-site registry with a larger N are more generalizable than the findings from a study with a smaller N or from a single center. Fortunately, many databases useful to toxicology researchers are de-identified and publicly available. Data used for studies from these types of databases are not considered human subjects’ research and do not require Institutional Review Board (IRB) review or approval. Depending on the source, data may even be provided in a format that can be readily analyzed using most commercially available statistical software, and in many cases, instructions for analysis are provided. However, it is important to recognize that some degree of statistical expertise and understanding of software is necessary to perform the analysis. For some databases, national-level data is available free of charge depending on the organization, while more detailed or local-level data may be available for a fee. Even when there is a cost to obtain the data, it is generally much less than collecting data on such a large scale by an individual researcher or group. These are reasons why large database research is time and cost-efficient. Although studies using large databases and registries are not perfect, many scholars have used them to generate meaningful hypotheses that set up next-step studies, and several researchers have successfully leveraged their large database study to support extramural funding applications. Choosing a Database The choice of database will depend on a variety of factors, but the primary factor to consider when selecting a database is the study question. Worthwhile topics include clinical manifestations in the setting of overdose, range of toxicity, treatment protocols, and adverse drug effects. In addition, there may be specific populations to be examined, such as age or gender, disease states, such as hepatic or renal impairment, or geographic trends. Different care settings (home poison center calls, emergency visits, hospitalizations, or addiction treatment center admissions) and short- or long-term outcomes (a single-acute care episode versus care over time) can also be studied. Once the study question has been established, the researcher should evaluate potential databases to determine if one or more is suitable to answer the study question before proceeding. Both toxicology-specific databases and more general databases may be used for a particular study. It is important to remember that no single registry or database, no matter how large the N , can be relied on to study every toxicology question. Examples of Toxicology-Specific Databases and Registries It is intuitive that toxicology investigators would turn to toxicology-specific databases and registries for research purposes. The data available generally has more details of interest to toxicology researchers than a broader, less specific database or registry. At the same time, toxicology-specific databases may not capture some exposures of interest. This section highlights representative examples in the USA; however, it should be noted that there are many similar databases and registries available internationally that can be utilized by toxicology researchers worldwide. National Poison Data System (NPDS) Most toxicology researchers are familiar with using the NPDS for research purposes, as it is intimately tied to the daily workings of America’s Poison Centers. NPDS offers decades of data with over two million cases reported each year, which allows for evaluation of a large number of cases involving a wide variety of toxicologic exposures, as well as trends over time. Information collected includes patient demographics, exposure details, therapy and level of care provided, and patient outcomes and disposition [ 1 ]. Investigators can examine data from a single poison center or use collective national-level data. Participating poison centers can access national-level data for free upon request; however, these data are not publicly available free of charge. Single-center studies can examine regional trends and allow for more detailed chart review; however, the generalizability of findings may be limited compared to national-level data. While NPDS data collection is standardized with continuous quality control and standardized definitions for key variables, the level of detail remains limited compared to a hospital-based medical chart review. More importantly, reporting to a poison center is voluntary and can be done by anyone. This means that NPDS data can underestimate disease burden and potentially bias NPDS toward more severe cases, as clinicians managing routine and less severe presentations may not report these to their regional poison center. Researchers should also remember that many exposures reported to poison centers come from non-clinician health staff and other members of the lay public, and there is no mechanism to verify the details entered into the large database [ 2 ]. NPDS studies generally require IRB review, but depending on the data collected, may be exempt. For example, if specific cases are to be reviewed from a regional center, then IRB approval would be required, whereas a study involving completely de-identified data from the national sample would be considered exempt [ 3 ]. If there is any question, it is prudent for a researcher to contact their IRB for guidance prior to beginning the study. Given the large number of cases and variables available in NPDS, statistical expertise and specific analytic programs may be required when analyzing NPDS data. Online Poison Control Data In the past decade, online poison control tools have emerged to address the public’s increasing use of the Internet as a source of health information, even in an emergency. The first of these products, webPOISONCONTROL®, is both an online triage tool and an app developed to provide triage and treatment recommendations for poison exposures [ 4 ]. Although developed for public use, webPOISONCONTROL®’s algorithms, substance database, and barcodes can be accessed through a separate interface by Specialists in Poison Information (SPIs) who are triaging poison exposure cases reported by telephone at participating poison centers. At the time of this writing, there are > 850,000 non-duplicated human exposures reported by the public in webPOISONCONTROL®, 70% of which are managed at home. Follow-up data are available for 30% of cases initially triaged to home [ 5 ]. Near-real-time webPOISONCONTROL® data (from 2017 to the present) are publicly available with more detailed local and national data available for analysis by participating U.S. poison centers. These data can be a significant source of poison control information and can be used to identify hazards and trends because as they are immediately available. They complement more traditional call center data by capturing cases from individuals who are unwilling or reluctant to call, possibly illuminating a different set of poisoning issues. At the same time, reporting is still voluntary, which would underestimate poisoning exposures, data are unverified as with calls to NPDS, and the population that uses the online platform may differ than those who prefer to call their regional poison center directly. Additionally, detailed follow-up data, particularly for patients who are referred to a healthcare facility are lacking, as webPOISONCONTROL® is primarily a triage tool. Publicly available data can be analyzed without IRB review, whereas more detailed data available to participating centers may require review and approval. Toxicology Investigators Consortium In addition to poison control data, the American College of Medical Toxicology (ACMT) oversees the Toxicology Investigators Consortium (ToxIC). This is a multi-center research group that collects data from patient encounters that involve bedside medical toxicology consultation—this is an important feature that distinguishes ToxIC from NPDS data where data are primarily obtained by specialists in poison information at regional poison centers remotely. The original core registry began in 2010, and there are currently 44 sites across the U.S., making the data collected geographically broad. There are additional detailed sub-registries that have received funding support from various sources, including the U.S. Food and Drug Administration, with additional sites that may or may not be part of the core registry [ 6 ]. The physician lead at each site is a medical toxicologist. Data collected include patient demographics and detailed information regarding the care provided by medical toxicology consultants, as well as treatment outcomes. There are standardized collection methods and quality review is performed. ToxIC can provide information on emerging exposure and treatment trends and could potentially be used to demonstrate the value of bedside medical toxicology consultation on patient outcomes and resource utilization. ToxIC does have some notable limitations, particularly that the data may not be reflective of broader practices at sites where bedside medical toxicology consultation is not available since most ToxIC sites are affiliated with a medical toxicology fellowship training program [ 6 ]. Importantly, entry into the ToxIC registry requires bedside medical toxicology consultation—those who receive consultation may be sicker than those who do not, which can be a potential source of bias. ToxIC data are not publicly available for researchers who are not affiliated with a study site. While there is a general IRB approval for the ToxIC registry, individual sites may require local IRB approval depending upon the details of the proposed study. Governmental Databases In addition to toxicology-specific databases and registries, many governmental agencies collect healthcare data that can be of use to a toxicology investigator. This section highlights some governmental databases in the U.S. that may be used for toxicology research. There are many similar databases that exist on an international level as well. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) The CDC produces both The National Ambulatory Medical Care Survey (NAMCS) and the National Hospital Ambulatory Medical Care Survey (NHAMCS). These are databases that provide information on the provision of care delivered in physician offices and hospital-based emergency departments (EDs), respectively. Due to the acute nature of the included visits, NHAMCS is more commonly used for toxicology research. NHAMCS is comprised of data collected by trained chart extractors using standardized methods and includes patient details, such as age, sex, gender, payer status, reason for visit, and diagnoses. Visit characteristics collected include resources used (laboratory and imaging studies, length of stay), medications administered and prescribed, and patient disposition. Hospital characteristics are also collected, including teaching status, urban location, and geographic region. Unlike NPDS or ToxIC where each reported case is counted, NHAMCS and other government-sponsored registries do not include all consecutive cases or exact counts but instead generate estimated numbers from rigorously defined representative samples. Each randomly selected emergency department is assigned a random 4-week reporting period, and weighted estimates are utilized to make national-level estimates [ 7 – 9 ]. While the survey is representative of trends in national ED visits, it has notable limitations, particularly that the data lacks the granularity of a detailed chart review. Specific dosing, medication administration route, and frequency are not available, limiting its utility for more detailed toxicologic research, such as investigating adverse drug effects. In addition, even with a large sample size, infrequent events do not have an adequate sample size to make reliable weighted estimates. The survey questionnaire changes annually based on continuous quality feedback and other policy needs, making it difficult to study some variables over time. The hospitals included in the survey change annually as well, which may create additional variability. While NAMCS has been less commonly used for toxicology research, it can potentially be used to examine outpatient prescribing trends and ambulatory care visits for patients with substance use disorder [ 7 , 8 ]. NHAMCS and NAMCS data are publicly available and de-identified; therefore, IRB review is not required. National-level data can be downloaded free of charge; however, more detailed local data are restricted. Healthcare Cost and Utilization Project (HCUP) HCUP is comprised of multiple databases, derived from billing data from all payers, that allow national, interstate, and intrastate evaluation of trends in healthcare utilization, access, charges, quality, and outcomes. Like NHAMCS and NAMCS, national-level estimates are generated from weighted samples. There are over 20 years of data for some of the databases; however, the information collected over time has changed periodically, making it difficult to evaluate some variables or trends. A toxicology researcher could study exposures in a variety of settings. HCUP offers the Nationwide Emergency Department Sample (NEDS), which can be used to examine ED visits, and there is also a robust inpatient sample of 35 million weighted visits that includes information on hospitalizations. Both can be queried to examine poisoning-related presentations. In addition, there is a separate database of pediatric hospital admissions, the Kids’ Inpatient Database (KID) that can be used to examine pediatric hospitalizations related to exposures, and another database for hospital readmissions that might be useful for toxicology researchers interested in examining cases of repeated exposures or return visits involving complications after initial hospitalization. See Table 1 for a summary of the different HCUP databases available. HCUP data can be used to study care, patient outcomes, and resource utilization over time and care settings. This can be useful in that it can examine an individual’s care over time and across care settings as opposed to a single ED visit or poison center chart; however, the data provided still does not have the same detail as an in-depth chart review. HCUP data access is gated with annual fees, and ambulatory patient information is limited [ 10 ]. The data provided are de-identified and therefore do not require IRB review. Table 1. Summary of HCUP databases of utility to toxicology researchers. Database Description National Inpatient Sample (NIS) -Regional and national estimates of inpatient utilization, access, cost, quality, and outcomes -Weighted estimate of 35 million hospitalizations nationally Kids’ Inpatient Database (KID) -National estimates of hospital inpatient stays for patients younger than 21 years of age; the unique design of the KID enables national and regional studies of common and rare pediatric conditions -Weighted estimate of 7 million hospitalizations Nationwide Emergency Department Sample (NEDS) -Large all-payer emergency department (ED) database in the USA -Weighted, estimate of 123 million ED visits Nationwide Readmissions Database (NRD) -Analyses of national readmissions for all patients, regardless of the expected payer for the hospital stay -Weighted estimate of 32 million discharges Open in a new tab Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA) SAMHSA is a branch of Health and Human Services dedicated to advancing behavioral health across the country. It has multiple information repositories from which one doing toxicologic research could benefit [ 11 ]. The Drug Abuse Warning Network (DAWN) collects electronic health record (EHR) data from participating hospitals from ED visits where a patient was treated for a substance-induced condition. DAWN monitors relevant demographic and geographic information that can be used to identify trends in the use and abuse of current and emerging recreational drugs. DAWN uses data from ten “sentinel” hospitals, which are large urban academic centers with significant morbidity and mortality attributed to opioid, cocaine, and psychostimulant overdose, but other hospitals are also included [ 12 ]. One of DAWN’s potential pitfalls is that it may miss medical-related visits that are complicated by substance use. Another arm of SAMHSA useful for toxicologic research is the National Survey on Drug Use and Health (SDUH), which provides estimates of substance use and mental health needs across national, state, and local strata [ 13 ]. SAMSHSA also collects data specific to addiction treatment in the National Survey of Substance Abuse Treatment Services (NSSATS). This database provides information of census levels at addiction treatment centers, along with geographic and utilization information [ 14 ]. The Treatment Episode Data Set (TEDS) is a database of treatment admissions that provides more patient-level data, with demographic information and details regarding substance abuse, including information about drugs used, route, age at first use, and prior treatment experience [ 15 ]. Additional information is provided in Table 2 . Table 2. Summary of SAMHSA databases of utility to toxicology researchers. Database Description Drug Abuse Warning Network (DAWN) -Nationwide public health surveillance on how emergency departments monitor substance use and visit trends Mental Health Services Survey (N-MHSS) -Information on the services and characteristics of mental health facilities in the USA National Survey on Drug Use and Health (NSDUH) -Information on mental health and use of tobacco, alcohol, and illicit drugs in the USA Mental Health Client-Level Data (MG-CLD) -Information on patients with mental health diagnoses, treatment services, and outcomes National Survey of Substance Abuse Treatment Services (N-SSATS) -Characteristics of substance use treatment facilities in the USA and services provided National Substance Use and Mental Health Services Survey (N-SUMHSS) -Information on substance use and mental health treatment facilities in the USA Treatment Episode Data Set (TEDS) -Admissions and discharge information from substance use treatment centers across the USA Open in a new tab Some of the data collected by SAMSHA is publicly available, while more detailed data is restricted. Because it is de-identified, research would not require IRB review. There are some limitations in that most of SAMHSA’s data is focused primarily on substance use and addiction, which potentially limits its utility in evaluating patients with primary medical diagnoses complicated by illicit xenobiotic use and toxicologic exposures not related to substance use or addiction. Although patient-specific information is lacking in this database, it is considered a surveillance system, and trends from DAWN have been used by local and national officials to set policy or prioritize resources [ 12 ]. As with other large datasets, the level of detail compared to a chart review is limited and the variables examined each year change periodically [ 11 ]. The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) FDA is responsible for ensuring safety within the realms of human and veterinary drugs, biologic agents such as vaccines, medical devices, food, cosmetics, and items that emit radiation. In accordance with its mission, the FDA collects information on adverse events associated with drugs and other products, medication error events, and other issues being monitored, given a safety concern as part of post-marketing surveillance. FDA has separate adverse event reporting databases for medications and vaccines [ 16 , 17 ]. Anyone is able to report an adverse event to the FDA, including healthcare professionals, patients, and manufacturers by filling out an online form. Reports are then evaluated by reviewers at the FDA and further investigation using other data sources may be undertaken if there is a safety concern identified. This process helps inform of potential safety hazards so that regulatory action may be taken if necessary. In addition, much of the data is publicly available online to the public. There is an interactive database as well as raw data files. Individual case reports can be requested by contacting the FDA. While these data can be helpful to the toxicology researcher interested in adverse drug events, there are some notable limitations. In many cases, adverse event reporting is voluntary, and known adverse events may not be reported at all, which can underestimate the frequency of occurrence. Less severe adverse effects may also go unreported. In addition, the quality of the information in the report may vary depending upon the training of the individual who filled out the form. Some reports may be duplicated, such as in the case when both a patient and a healthcare provider both report the same event. Because the data are de-identified and publicly available, IRB review is not necessary. While the interactive database is user-friendly, analysis of the raw data files will require some degree of statistical expertise [ 16 ]. Other Large Databases and Registries Claims Data Medicaid/Medicare and private insurance companies are another potentially useful source of data for toxicology research. Pharmacy claims data can also be reviewed. There are also integrated databases. One example is from IBM MarketScan [ 18 ]. This is a de-identified patient-level database with information contributed by multiple sources, including Medicare, Medicaid, private insurers, large employers, hospitals, and managed care organizations. There is information about patients, including demographics and health-related encounters, and pharmacy claims data. In addition, there is information about hospitalizations, workplace absence, and compensation and disability claims. This could enable a researcher to examine the longitudinal delivery of healthcare to a family and the costs and outcomes associated with that care in a variety of settings. Claims data does have its limitations, as it does not capture uninsured people or those who pay out of pocket, and when a person changes insurance, their trail of claims may not be captured. In addition, public access to data may be limited without a subscription. As with any large database, statistical expertise is required for analysis. When using de-identified data, IRB review would not be required [ 18 ]. Health Systems Data Toxicology investigators can use data from an individual health system or collaborate to examine data from a group of health systems. Sample sizes may be large depending upon the size of the system and the frequency of the presentation being studied. Even though numbers can be large, there is also the possibility to access and review individual charts as needed. Local trends can be analyzed, which may be more applicable to the researcher’s daily practice. Additionally, research performed can be used to inform quality improvement initiatives within the health system. There are some limitations in using health system data in that information pulled from an electronic health record may not be in a format that is easy to analyze. IRB approval is required if charts are being accessed, and unless the particular system has a broad geographic presence, information may not be generalizable. Limitations of Large Databases and Registries There are important limitations to be aware of when performing research with large databases and registries. Even though the sample size is large, evaluation of rare events or presentations still may be limited. Because the data is often already collected, the individual researcher has little or no control over what level of detail or what specific data points are collected. They must work with what is available to them. Large databases only allow for evaluations of trends over time if it is a longitudinal acquisition, or a prospectively collected set, and variables may change over time. In addition, researchers who use these databases are typically given de-identified data and are unable to access individual charts to obtain more detail as can be done with smaller N or single-center chart review studies. Researchers should also know that data from some sources is not publicly available until a year or more after collection because of all the processing steps and quality checks involved by the sponsor of the registry or database. As such, the information used by the researcher may not be reflective of current and emerging toxicology trends. Because each dataset includes different variables and has different sampling methods, it may be difficult to compare certain data across databases. While some information is provided to the public for free, there is often a fee for more detailed data, and many insurance companies, health systems, and other agencies do not publicly share at all. Given the large sample size and different data sampling and storage methods, experience with software programs for appropriate statistical analysis or engaging the assistance of a qualified statistician early in study design are required. Getting Started Once a specific research question has been developed, and a potential database or registry is identified, the toxicology investigator should familiarize themselves with their chosen source of data. Solid registry or database study design requires an understanding of the survey items and data collection methods and limitations. It can be helpful to read previous publications using that specific dataset and to network with other investigators who have used the same database for their research projects. Many of the database and registry websites include a list of recent publications—reading these is strongly encouraged, and contacting the corresponding author of a published study may result in additional free advice or even a helpful collaboration. It is also important to read the limitations section of previously published papers using some of these large sets of data. This will help to refine your study question, allow for more meaningful analysis and interpretation of the data, and candid recognition of limitations. Because these are very large datasets, specific software expertise and skills for statistical analysis are required. Engaging a statistician as a valuable part of the study team should be done early. Conclusions There are many large databases and registries available to medical toxicologists for research purposes. The contribution of these databases can be significant, given the logistical and ethical considerations in performing randomized controlled trials in poisoned patients. Investigators should familiarize themselves with the strengths and limitations of each database in answering specific study questions and how to interpret the data available to further advance knowledge in the field of medical toxicology. Funding None. Declarations Conflict of Interest Dr. Amirshahi works on webPOISONCONTROL as part of her employment at the National Capital Poison Center, which is discussed in this manuscript. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Gummin DD, Mowry JB, Beuhler MC, Spyker DA, Rivers LJ, Feldman R, Brown K, Nathaniel PTP, Bronstein AC, Weber JA. 2021 Annual Report of the National Poison Data System © (NPDS) from America’s Poison Centers: 39th Annual Report. Clin Toxicol (Phila). 2022;60(12):1381–643. 10.1080/15563650.2022.2132768 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Hoffman RS. Understanding the limitations of retrospective analyses of poison center data. Clin Toxicol (Phila). 2007;45(8):943–5. 10.1080/15563650701233370 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. 2018 Common Rule. https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/regulations-and-policy/regulations/45-cfr-46/revised-common-rule-regulatory-text/index.html . Accessed 29 Jan 2024. 4. webPOISONCONTROL ® . https://www.webpoisoncontrol.org . Accessed 7 Feb 2024. 5. Reid NE, Johnson-Arbor K, Smolinske S, Litovitz T. 2020 webPOISONCONTROL data summary. Am J Emerg Med. 2022;54:184–95. 10.1016/j.ajem.2022.02.014 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Toxicology Investigators Consortium. https://www.acmt.net/toxic/ . Accessed 2 Oct 2023. 7. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Ambulatory care data. https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/ahcd/index.htm . Accessed 12 Oct 2023. 8. McCaig LF, Burt CW. Understanding and interpreting the National Hospital Ambulatory Medical Care Survey: key questions and answers. Ann Emerg Med. 2012;60(6):716-721.e1. 10.1016/j.annemergmed.2012.07.010 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. National Healthcare Surveys Geocodes. https://www.cdc.gov/rdc/geocodes/geowt_nhcs.htm . Accessed 14 Dec 2023. 10. AHRQ. Healthcare Cost and Utilization Project User Support. https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov . Accessed 13 Oct 2023. 11. Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration. Data We Collect. https://www.samhsa.gov/data/data-we-collect . Accessed 13 Oct 2023. 12. Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration. Drug Abuse Warning Network. https://www.samhsa.gov/data/data-we-collect/dawn-drug-abuse-warning-network . Accessed 18 Dec 2023. 13. Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration. National Survey on Drug Use and Health. https://www.samhsa.gov/data/data-we-collect/nsduh-national-survey-drug-use-and-health . Accessed 18 Dec 2023. 14. Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration. National Survey on Substance Abuse Treatment Services. https://www.samhsa.gov/data/data-we-collect/n-ssats-national-survey-substance-abuse-treatment-services . Accessed 18 Dec 2023. 15. Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration. Treatment Episode Data Set. https://www.samhsa.gov/data/data-we-collect/teds-treatment-episode-data-set . Accessed 18 Dec 2023. 16. FDA Adverse Event Reporting System. https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/fda-adverse-event-reporting-system-faers . Accessed 13 Oct 2023. 17. Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting