INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4202,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10623189','Feasibility of Using the Privacy-preserving Large Language Model Vicuna for Labeling Radiology Reports.','Mukherjee P, Hou B, Lanfredi RB, Summers RM.','2023','13b multistep chexbert labelers hyperparameter randomness labelers labelers chexbert chexbert labelers labelers 13b labelers 13b labeler undisclosed nontrivial infeasible forgo tedious alpaca alpaca davinci alpaca emulate 13b chestx ray14 labeler chexbert deaconess chestx ray14 13b labeler chexbert chexbert 13b enlg cardiomed cardiomediastinum chexbert labeler labeler cardiomegaly cardiomediastinum cardiomegaly cardiomediastinum cardiomediastinum heuristically javascript notation json json chexbert labelers hyperparameter randomness randomness randomness interrun chexbert labelers json desctools purrr outputted json json json json hyperparameter javascript notation json hyperparameter json chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert cardiomediastinum chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert cardiomediastinum chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert cardiomegaly randomness cardiomediastinum chexbert cardiomediastinum chexbert chexbert chexbert multistep cardiomediastinum cxb chexbert labeler cxl labeler picc va1 va2 13b chexbert chexbert chexbert chexbert hyperparameter chexbert chexbert cardiomediastinum chexbert 13b chexbert json json chexbert multistep hyperparameter randomness multistep randomness randomness json chexbert negate 13b chexbert ninet sinaii biowulf pingan pingan scanmed holdings mbg json javascript notation haver ambinder bahl oluyemi jeudy e230424 truhn e230725 steinkamp pomeranz adleberg e190137 touvron lavril izacard taori gulrajani alpaca tatsu stanford_alpaca impressing lmsys kordi galley aew bagheri chestx ray8 cvpr irvin aaai pareek lungren labelers aclanthology emnlp tienet cvf holste callı sogancioglu ginneken meedeniya kumarasinghe kolonne biom mozayan maneevese tocino chheang bagheri negbio negation summits tejani rayan e220007 oakden cicchetti paradoxes','Abstract Background Large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT, though proficient in many text-based tasks, are not suitable for use with radiology reports due to patient privacy constraints. Purpose To test the feasibility of using an alternative LLM (Vicuna-13B) that can be run locally for labeling radiography reports. Materials and Methods Chest radiography reports from the MIMIC-CXR and National Institutes of Health (NIH) data sets were included in this retrospective study. Reports were examined for 13 findings. Outputs reporting the presence or absence of the 13 findings were generated by Vicuna by using a single-step or multistep prompting strategy (prompts 1 and 2, respectively). Agreements between Vicuna outputs and CheXpert and CheXbert labelers were assessed using Fleiss κ. Agreement between Vicuna outputs from three runs under a hyperparameter setting that introduced some randomness (temperature, 0.7) was also assessed. The performance of Vicuna and the labelers was assessed in a subset of 100 NIH reports annotated by a radiologist with use of area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC). Results A total of 3269 reports from the MIMIC-CXR data set (median patient age, 68 years [IQR, 59–79 years]; 161 male patients) and 25 596 reports from the NIH data set (median patient age, 47 years [IQR, 32–58 years]; 1557 male patients) were included. Vicuna outputs with prompt 2 showed, on average, moderate to substantial agreement with the labelers on the MIMIC-CXR (κ median, 0.57 [IQR, 0.45–0.66] with CheXpert and 0.64 [IQR, 0.45–0.68] with CheXbert) and NIH (κ median, 0.52 [IQR, 0.41–0.65] with CheXpert and 0.55 [IQR, 0.41–0.74] with CheXbert) data sets, respectively. Vicuna with prompt 2 performed at par (median AUC, 0.84 [IQR, 0.74–0.93]) with both labelers on nine of 11 findings. Conclusion In this proof-of-concept study, outputs of the LLM Vicuna reporting the presence or absence of 13 findings on chest radiography reports showed moderate to substantial agreement with existing labelers. © RSNA, 2023 Supplemental material is available for this article. See also the editorial by Cai in this issue. Open in a new tab Summary The large language model Vicuna-13B, which can be run locally without de-identification of patient data, assigned labels to chest radiography reports from two data sets, with moderate to substantial agreement with existing labelers. Key Results ■ In this retrospective study, Vicuna-13B outputs reporting the presence or absence of 13 findings in chest radiography reports showed moderate agreement with CheXpert labeler outputs on the MIMIC-CXR ( n = 3269; κ median, 0.57 [IQR, 0.45–0.66]) and National Institutes of Health (NIH) ( n = 25 596; κ median, 0.52) data sets. ■ In 100 NIH reports with radiologist annotations, Vicuna (median area under the receiver operating characteristic curve [AUC], 0.84) performed at par with CheXpert (median AUC, 0.91) for nine of 11 findings. Introduction Recently released general-purpose large language models (LLMs), such as ChatGPT and GPT-4, have garnered substantial attention due to their remarkable ability to perform language generation and language understanding tasks, including question answering and conversion of free text to structured formats. For example, ChatGPT can provide accurate responses to questions about breast cancer prevention and screening ( 1 ). Furthermore, GPT-4 is capable of transforming free-text CT and MRI radiology reports into structured reports and can also identify key findings in chest radiography reports ( 2 ). This indicates that general-purpose LLMs can perform well on text-based radiology-related tasks without task-specific training. At the time of writing, both ChatGPT and GPT-4 are proprietary models that require the user to send the data to OpenAI for processing using either the chat interface or an application programming interface. In addition, these models are very large (ChatGPT has 175 billion parameters, and the size of GPT-4, while still undisclosed, is expected to be larger) and computationally expensive, requiring multiple graphics processing units for inference, even if they were available to run locally. Thus, despite their promise, these models cannot be used with radiology reports in a clinical setting without first de-identifying the patient data. Unfortunately, removing all patient health information and personal identifiable information from radiology reports is a nontrivial task. Manual de-identification is infeasible for large sets of reports, and fully automated methods often do not perform well enough to forgo tedious manual review ( 3 ). Identifying alternative publicly available LLMs that can be run locally in a privacy-preserving manner is thus warranted. Fortunately, several trained LLMs have been made publicly available recently, including a base model called LLaMA and its fine-tuned variants, such as Alpaca and Vicuna ( 4 – 6 ). While LLaMA is pretrained to predict the next word (token) given a context, Alpaca and Vicuna were fine-tuned in a supervised manner using an existing strong LLM (text-davinci-003 for Alpaca and ChatGPT for Vicuna). A set of instruction prompts and the corresponding outputs obtained from the strong LLM were used to fine-tune LLaMA, and thus, Vicuna tries to emulate ChatGPT''s output to an input prompt ( 7 , 8 ). Despite it having only 13 billion parameters, far fewer parameters compared with either ChatGPT or GPT-4, preliminary evaluation has shown that Vicuna approaches the performance of ChatGPT in tasks such as multilingual question answering ( 8 ). Thus, the purpose of this proof-of-concept study was to evaluate the feasibility of using a locally run LLM, Vicuna-13B (hereafter, Vicuna), to label key findings on chest radiography reports from the MIMIC-CXR data set (hereafter, MIMIC) ( 9 ) and the National Institutes of Health (NIH) ChestX-ray14 data set (hereafter, NIH data set) ( 10 ) and compare the performance of Vicuna with that of other widely used methods, including CheXpert labeler ( 11 ) and CheXbert ( 12 ). Materials and Methods This retrospective study was approved by the local institutional review board, and the requirement for written informed consent was waived. Study Data Sets This study used publicly available free-text reports from the MIMIC data set ( 9 ) and internal reports corresponding with publicly available images from the NIH data set ( 10 ) ( Fig 1 ). The MIMIC data set contains 227 827 free-text radiology reports obtained from the Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center in Boston, Massachusetts. Details on how this data set was generated have been reported previously ( 9 ). The official test set, which consists of 3269 reports, was used in the current study. The NIH data set contains 112 120 images from 30 805 patients, among which 25 596 images belong to the official test set. The 25 596 reports (which are not publicly available) corresponding to the images in the official test set were used as the data set in this study ( 10 ). Figure 1: Open in a new tab Flowchart shows the data selection process from the (A) MIMIC-CXR and (B) National Institutes of Health (NIH) ChestX-ray14 data sets. Because MIMIC is a publicly available data set, numerous studies in the existing literature have used it; however, to the best of our knowledge, no study has used it to assess the performance of general-purpose LLMs as done herein. The NIH chest radiography reports have been used to study paired image and text embedding ( 13 ) and recently in a study on the long-tailed classification of thoracic diseases at chest radiography, in which the reports were used to provide binary labels for several additional findings ( 14 ). Models The LLM evaluated in this study was Vicuna-13B ( 6 ), version 0, which was created by supervised instruction fine-tuning on a version of the foundational LLM LLaMA using approximately 70 000 user-shared ChatGPT conversations ( 4 ). Of the open-source LLMs available, Vicuna was chosen because preliminary evaluations have reported that it performs comparably with ChatGPT ( 8 ). The Vicuna model was used as is, with no additional training or adjustments. Vicuna was compared with two existing models: the natural language processing rule-based CheXpert labeler ( 11 ) and CheXbert, a Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers, or BERT, model trained on a combination of CheXpert labels and human annotations ( 12 ). In the machine learning literature, data labeled using CheXpert labels have been referred to as “gold standard data,” and this labeling technique has been used in a large number of studies, particularly for image classification tasks ( 15 ). CheXbert, however, has been shown to outperform CheXpert when evaluated with reference-standard human annotations ( 12 ). An overview of the study is shown in Figure 2 . Figure 2: Open in a new tab Overview of the study. (A) The open-access large language model Vicuna-13B, which can be run on a local computer without the need for de-identification of patient data, was prompted to examine unstructured, free-text chest radiography (CXR) reports and generate an output file reporting the results of 13 specific findings. AP = anteroposterior, Enlg. Cardiomed. = enlarged cardiomediastinum, PNA = pneumonia. (B) Reports from the MIMIC-CXR data set ( n = 3269) and the National Institutes of Health (NIH) data set ( n = 25 596) were used in this study. Vicuna was given two independent tasks that generated two different output files, one in which the 13 possible findings were labeled as positive or negative (task 2, orange model) and the other in which the 13 possible findings were labeled as positive, negative, unsure, or not mentioned (task 1, yellow model). The agreement between Vicuna model outputs and the CheXbert labeler, CheXpert labeler, and human annotations were compared using Fleiss or Cohen κ as appropriate. Classification Tasks and Prompts Chest radiography reports were assessed for the following 13 CheXpert-based findings: “atelectasis,” “cardiomegaly,” “consolidation,” “edema,” “enlarged cardiomediastinum,” “fracture,” “lung lesion,” “lung opacity,” “pleural effusion,” “pleural other,” “pneumonia,” “pneumothorax,” and “support devices” ( 11 ). We excluded the “no finding” label because it is derived from the other labels: If none of the 12 pathologic findings (excluding support devices) are assigned a positive or uncertain label, CheXpert labels the report with “no finding.” Note that these labels are not independent of each other. For example, cardiomegaly implies enlarged cardiomediastinum, and consolidation, edema, lung lesion, or pneumonia can all imply lung opacity. For task 1, the Vicuna model was prompted to categorize the 13 possible findings as positive (value of 1), negative (0), not mentioned (NA), or unsure (−1). Herein, positive denotes that the patient has the finding, negative denotes that the patient does not have the finding, and unsure denotes that while the finding or its context is mentioned in the report, a definitive determination as to the presence or absence of the finding cannot be made. For example, a follow-up report that mentions that the cardiomediastinum is unchanged from a previous report would be categorized as “unsure,” as it cannot be determined if it is enlarged or not. Finally, the label is categorized as “not mentioned” if pathology is not mentioned in the report. Task 2 is a simplified version of task 1, where the “not mentioned” and “unsure” categories from task 1 were collapsed into a single negative category, and the Vicuna model was prompted to categorize the 13 possible findings as either positive or negative. Heuristically, this corresponds to the scenario where we are interested in identifying reports that are definitely positive for a finding. Such binary labels are often used in practice for training models to classify chest radiographs ( 16 ). Two English-language prompts were independently used to instruct the model to classify the reports as required for either task 1 or task 2. Prompt 1 was a slightly modified version of the prompt used in reference 2—essentially, it asks the model to provide an output in a valid JavaScript object notation (JSON) format, with each of the 13 findings as a key and the corresponding output class (0, 1, −1, or NA for task 1 and 0 or 1 for task 2) as the value. After experience was gained with the Vicuna model''s response to various types of prompting, prompt 2 was developed, which uses a rule-based interactive prompting strategy ( Fig S1 ). Instead of asking for the classes of all findings simultaneously, it asks the model to answer simpler yes-or-no questions about the presence or absence of individual findings in the report, and the final class label for a finding is assigned based on the outputs of the model to each prompt. Thus, prompt 2 did not require outputs in a valid JSON format. Additional details about the two prompts can be found in Appendix S1 . Evaluations and Statistical Analyses To assess if the outputs of Vicuna with prompt 1 and Vicuna with prompt 2 agree with the outputs obtained from the CheXpert and the CheXbert labelers, the Fleiss κ statistic ( 17 ) was used. The strength of agreement for κ values is as follows: less than 0 = poor, 0–0.20 = slight, 0.21–0.40 = fair, 0.41–0.60 = moderate, 0.61–0.80 = substantial, and 0.81–1.00 = almost perfect ( 18 ). This analysis was carried out in a pairwise manner between outputs generated by two models assessing reports from both the NIH and MIMIC data sets, and false discovery rate was used to correct for multiple comparisons ( 19 ). Comparisons of agreement between two sets of methods were made using the Wilcoxon signed-rank test considering the corresponding κ values of each finding as a paired sample ( 20 ). By default, generative LLMs such as Vicuna can give different outputs to the same prompt in two different runs. The “temperature” hyperparameter controls this randomness, with a high temperature adding more randomness. For this analysis, Vicuna outputs were obtained using a temperature setting of 0, thus removing the randomness. To evaluate if the outputs of Vicuna are sensitive to the temperature setting, task 2 was run three times using the NIH reports on Vicuna with its default temperature setting of 0.7. Fleiss κ was used to evaluate the agreement between the three runs. To further explore the interrun variability of outputs, this was repeated for two other temperature settings: 0.35 and 1. Next, the majority vote of the three runs at the default temperature setting of 0.7 was determined and compared with the output of Vicuna with a temperature setting of 0 using Cohen κ ( 21 ) and Cramér V statistics. Finally, the comparative reliability of Vicuna, CheXpert, and CheXbert labelers were assessed using a subset of 100 reports randomly sampled without replacement from the NIH test set. The reports were manually reviewed by a board-certified senior radiologist (R.M.S., with more than 25 years of experience) specializing in thoracic and gastrointestinal radiology and body cross-sectional imaging and annotated according to the CheXpert labeling rules. Only 11 of the 13 possible findings were assessed in this analysis, as “pneumonia” and “fracture” each only had a single true-positive finding ( Table S1 ). Task 2 was the focus, and a receiver operating characteristic analysis of each model was performed using the human annotations as the ground truth labels. P values were obtained based on the null hypothesis that the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) equals 0.5 (ie, the model performs no better than random chance) using the Wilcoxon rank-sum test ( 20 ). The performance of the models on task 2 (positive or negative finding) was compared in a pairwise manner using the AUC, and the DeLong test was used to infer if differences in AUC were significant ( 22 ). In prompt 1, the model was instructed to output the labels in a specific JSON format. If the output for a report was not in the requested format, it was counted as a missing value. Missing values were dropped when Cramér V was computed. All statistical analyses were carried out by an author (P.M.) in R (version 4.2.1, The R Foundation) using the packages “irr” (version 0.84.1), “DescTools” (version 0.99.46), “purrr” (version 0.3.4), “verification” (version 1.42), and “pROC” (version 1.18.0). P < .05 was considered indicative of a statistically significant difference. Results Patient Characteristics From the NIH data set, 25 596 reports from 2797 patients (median age, 47 years [IQR, 32–58 years]; 1557 male and 1240 female patients) were used for all analyses except for model performance comparisons, for which 100 reports from 92 patients were used (median age, 48 years [IQR, 34–58 years]; 55 male and 37 female patients) ( Fig 1 ). From the MIMIC data set, 3269 reports from 293 patients (median age, 68 years [IQR, 59–79 years]; 161 male and 132 female patients) were used for analyses. Valid Vicuna Outputs With prompt 1 and task 1, Vicuna outputted valid JSON files with no missing labels for 25 543 of 25 596 NIH reports (99.8%) and 3267 of 3269 MIMIC reports (99.9%). For task 2, 23 883 of 25 596 NIH reports (93.3%) and 3014 of 3269 MIMIC reports (92.2%) had valid JSON outputs from Vicuna. Increasing the temperature led to a decrease in the percentage of valid JSON outputs ( Fig 3A ). When the model temperature was set at the default value of 0.7, the percentage of valid JSON outputs for task 2 decreased from 93.3% (23 883 of 25 596 reports) to an average of 79.6% (20 359, 20 366, and 20 421 of 25 596 reports over three runs; P < .001). Figure 3: Open in a new tab Effect of the temperature hyperparameter. (A) Line graph shows that the proportion of valid JavaScript object notation (JSON) outputs obtained from Vicuna using prompt 1 on the National Institutes of Health data set decreased as the temperature hyperparameter increased. At each of the three temperatures of 0.35, 0.7, and 1, three points are plotted showing the actual proportion of the valid JSON outputs for the three runs. The horizontal lines mark the medians of the three points for each temperature. (B) Box and whisker plots of the degree of agreement measured with use of Fleiss κ among three runs of Vicuna show significant decreases as the temperature increases for both prompt 1 and prompt 2. The numbers near the top of the plot represent P values computed using the Wilcoxon signed-rank test. In this plot, the midline represents the median, and box edges represent the first and third quartiles. The whiskers represent the range of the data, excluding outliers. The outliers are points that are beyond [median − 1.5 × IQR, median + 1.5 × IQR]. Agreement between Models for Task 1 Using the MIMIC data set, CheXpert and CheXbert showed almost perfect agreement for all 13 findings (κ median, 0.90 [IQR, 0.84–0.94]) ( Table 1 ). However, outputs for the 13 findings using Vicuna with prompt 1 showed, on average, poor agreement (less agreement than expected by chance) with the CheXbert model (κ median, −0.40 [IQR, −0.50 to −0.11]), CheXpert model (κ median, −0.42 [IQR, −0.51 to −0.11]), and Vicuna with prompt 2 (κ median, −0.18 [IQR, −0.30 to −0.05]). On the other hand, with prompt 2, Vicuna showed significantly better agreement ( P < .001) with CheXbert and CheXpert: It was fair (κ median, 0.40 [IQR, 0.37–0.49]) with CheXbert and moderate (κ median, 0.41 [IQR, 0.35–0.48]) with CheXpert. The agreement of Vicuna with CheXpert and CheXbert was highest for the finding of edema (κ = 0.57 [95% CI: 0.55, 0.59] and 0.58 [95% CI: 0.56, 0.61], respectively), while it was poorest for the finding of pneumothorax (κ = −0.01 [95% CI: −0.04, 0.02]) and enlarged cardiomediastinum (κ = −0.01 [95% CI: −0.04, 0.02]). Table 1: Pairwise Agreement between CheXpert, CheXbert, and Vicuna Task 1 Output for 13 Findings on Chest Radiography Reports Open in a new tab Using the NIH data set, CheXpert and CheXbert showed, on average, almost perfect agreement (κ median, 0.89 [IQR, 0.74–0.91]). As in MIMIC, Vicuna with prompt 1 showed poor agreement with both CheXbert (κ median, −0.42 [IQR, −0.70 to −0.20]) and CheXpert (κ median, −0.48 [IQR, −0.71 to −0.21]). Vicuna with prompt 2 showed significantly better agreement ( P < .001) with both CheXbert and CheXpert: The agreement was fair with both CheXbert (κ median, 0.31 [IQR, 0.18–0.38]) and CheXpert (κ median, 0.36 [IQR, 0.17–0.40]). With prompt 2, the agreement of Vicuna with CheXbert and CheXpert was highest for the finding of pleural effusion (κ = 0.70 [95% CI: 0.69, 0.71] and 0.68 [95% CI: 0.67, 0.69], respectively), while it was poorest for the finding of enlarged cardiomediastinum (κ = 0.05 [95% CI: 0.04, 0.05] and 0.03 [95% CI: 0.02, 0.04], respectively). Finally, the 13 label outputs of Vicuna with prompt 1 showed poor agreement, on average, with the 13 label outputs of Vicuna with prompt 2 (κ median, −0.36 [IQR, −0.62 to 0.04]). Agreement between Models for Task 2 CheXpert and CheXbert showed almost perfect agreement in both the MIMIC (κ median, 0.89 [IQR, 0.84–0.93]) and NIH (κ median, 0.89 [IQR, 0.79–0.91]) data sets ( Table 2 ). Unlike in task 1, Vicuna with prompt 1 had, on average, moderate agreement with CheXbert on both the MIMIC (κ median, 0.56 [IQR, 0.31–0.60]) and NIH data sets (κ median, 0.51 [IQR, 0.20–0.70]) data sets. Similarly, it had moderate agreement with CheXpert on the MIMIC (κ median, 0.46 [IQR, 0.31–0.60]) and NIH (κ median, 0.53 [IQR, 0.12–0.60]) reports. With prompt 2, Vicuna showed substantial agreement with CheXbert (κ median, 0.64 [IQR, 0.45–0.68]) and moderate agreement with CheXpert (κ median, 0.57 [IQR, 0.45–0.66]) on the MIMIC data set. Compared with prompt 1, prompt 2 led to significantly better agreement with CheXbert ( P = .02) and CheXpert ( P = .02) on MIMIC reports. On the NIH reports, Vicuna with prompt 2 showed moderate agreement with both CheXbert (κ median, 0.55 [IQR, 0.41–0.74]) and CheXpert (κ median, 0.52 [IQR, 0.41–0.65]); the difference between using prompt 1 and prompt 2 was not significant ( P = .15 for CheXbert and P = .13 for CheXpert). Finally, the agreement between Vicuna with prompt 1 and Vicuna with prompt 2 was substantial on the MIMIC reports (κ median, 0.64 [IQR, 0.34–0.75]) and moderate on the NIH reports (κ median, 0.47 [IQR, 0.28–0.59]). Table 2: Pairwise Agreement between CheXpert, CheXbert, and Vicuna Task 2 Output for 13 Findings on Chest Radiography Reports Open in a new tab Impact of Temperature on Model Agreement Increasing the temperature led to a decrease in agreement among three runs on the NIH data set ( Fig 3B ). With the temperature set to Vicuna''s default value of 0.7, the 13 findings from three runs using the NIH data showed substantial agreement with prompt 1 (κ median, 0.79 [IQR, 0.61–0.81]) and moderate agreement with prompt 2 (κ median, 0.55 [IQR, 0.48–0.58]) ( Table 3 ). Furthermore, the majority vote for each of the 13 findings from the three runs showed almost perfect and substantial agreement with the findings generated using the temperature setting of 0 for prompt 1 (κ median, 0.82 [IQR, 0.71–0.89]) and prompt 2 (κ median, 0.79 [IQR, 0.71–0.80]), respectively, and their differences were not statistically significant ( P = .68). The Cramér V between the majority vote finding and the findings generated with a temperature setting of 0 ranged from 0.47 (95% CI: 0.46, 0.49) for pleural other to 0.95 (95% CI: 0.93, 0.96) for pneumothorax using prompt 1 (median, 0.83 [IQR, 0.72–0.89]) and from 0.54 (95% CI: 0.53, 0.56) for edema to 0.88 (95% CI: 0.87, 0.89) for cardiomegaly and pleural effusion using prompt 2 (median, 0.79 [IQR, 0.72–0.81]), respectively, with no statistically significant differences between prompts 1 and 2 ( P = .74) ( Table S2 ). Table 3: Agreement between Task 2 Outputs from Vicuna Runs with Different Randomness Parameters Open in a new tab Comparison of Model Performance For task 2, Vicuna with prompt 2 had a higher AUC than with prompt 1 for nine of 11 findings ( Table 4 ); however, the differences were significant only for findings of pleural other ( P = .007) and support devices ( P < .001). Vicuna with prompt 2 significantly outperformed CheXpert on the finding of enlarged cardiomediastinum (AUC, 0.79 vs 0.49; P < .001), while CheXpert had a significantly higher AUC than Vicuna on the finding of support devices (0.91 vs 0.73; P < .001); the differences in AUC for the other findings were not significant. Like CheXpert, CheXbert also had a significantly higher AUC than Vicuna (with prompt 2) for the finding of support devices (0.97 vs 0.73; P < .001), while Vicuna performed significantly better for the finding of enlarged cardiomediastinum (0.79 vs 0.53; P = .002). The majority vote finding following three runs of 100 reports from the NIH data set on Vicuna with the default temperature of 0.7 had lower AUC than that of the single run with a temperature setting of 0 for all 11 findings when prompt 2 was used (temperature of 0.7 median AUC, 0.65 [IQR, 0.60–0.71] vs temperature of 0 median AUC, 0.84 [IQR, 0.74–0.93]; P = .002), though the difference was not significant for the findings of consolidation ( P = .62) and pleural other ( P = .41). For prompt 1, the majority vote had significantly higher AUC for findings of atelectasis (0.57 vs 0; P < .001) and lung lesion (0.69 vs 0; P < .001) while performing significantly worse on edema (0 vs 0.99; P < .001); the differences in AUC for the other findings were not significant. Examples of how reports were labeled by CheXpert, CheXbert, Vicuna, and the radiologist are shown in Figure 4 . Performance results based on the binary and macro-averaged F1 score metric are presented in Tables S3 and S4 , respectively. Table 4: Performance of CheXpert, CheXbert, and Vicuna Task 2 Output for 13 Findings on a Subset of Chest Radiography Reports from the National Institutes of Health Data Set ( n = 100) Open in a new tab Figure 4: Open in a new tab Example chest radiography reports and findings identified by CheXpert, CheXbert, Vicuna, and a radiologist. Both prompt 1 (a single-step prompt) and prompt 2 (a multistep interactive rule-based prompt) were used to run task 2 (positive or negative finding) on the large language model Vicuna. A value of 1 indicates a positive finding, while 0 indicates a negative finding. Values in blue represent outputs that agreed with the findings of the radiologist (Rad) (numbers in black), and red values represent outputs that disagreed with the findings of the radiologist. Key phrases in the reports are underlined. ap = anteroposterior, CM = cardiomediastinum, CXB = CheXbert labeler, CXL = CheXpert labeler, et = endotracheal, exam = examination, picc = peripherally inserted central catheter, VA1 = Vicuna run with prompt 1, VA2 = Vicuna run with prompt 2. Discussion In this exploratory study, we assessed the feasibility of using a locally run large language model, Vicuna-13B, to label and identify 13 specific findings on chest radiography reports from two large data sets and compared its outputs and performance with two widely used labeling tools, CheXpert and CheXbert. On task 1, Vicuna with prompt 1 showed poor to slight agreement with the CheXbert (κ median, −0.42 [IQR, −0.70 to −0.20]) and CheXpert (κ median, −0.48 [IQR, −0.71 to −0.21]) models on the National Institutes of Health (NIH) data set. With prompt 2, the agreement improved significantly ( P < .001) with both CheXbert (κ median, 0.31 [IQR, 0.18–0.38]) and CheXpert (κ median, 0.36 [IQR, 0.17–0.40]). On task 2, Vicuna with both prompt 1 and prompt 2 showed moderate to substantial agreement with CheXbert (κ median, 0.56 [IQR, 0.31–0.60] for prompt 1 and 0.64 [IQR, 0.45–0.68] for prompt 2) and CheXpert (κ median, 0.46 [IQR, 0.31–0.60] for prompt 1 and 0.57 [IQR, 0.45–0.66] for prompt 2) on the MIMIC data set. We also found that increasing the temperature hyperparameter decreased agreement among three inference runs of Vicuna with both prompt 1 and prompt 2. Finally, human evaluation with a subset of 100 NIH reports found no significant difference in the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) performance of Vicuna with prompt 2 and CheXpert or CheXbert for most findings; Vicuna significantly outperformed CheXpert (AUC, 0.79 vs 0.49; P < .001) and CheXbert (0.79 vs 0.53; P = .002) on the finding of enlarged cardiomediastinum, while CheXpert and CheXbert had a significantly higher AUC than Vicuna on the finding of support devices (0.91 vs 0.73 [ P < .001] and 0.97 vs 0.73 [ P < .001], respectively). Various natural language processing approaches have been adopted in the past to extract information and identify findings on radiology reports, including rule-based methods and deep learning–based language models ( 11 , 23 – 25 ). The rule-based methods require extensive work to come up with suitable rules and yet are often far from perfect ( 26 ). The deep learning models also require substantial effort: human annotations for reference-standard labels and complicated model training. Pretrained LLMs, such as ChatGPT and GPT-4, may have notable advantages: They may require no extra training, lack the need to create complex rules, and can be easily instructed in plain language by non–computer scientists. Indeed, GPT-4 was recently shown to be able to structure radiology reports based on a template and perform at par with existing models on a set of German-language radiology reports ( 2 ). Unfortunately, GPT-4 cannot be easily used with radiology reports due to privacy constraints. Therefore, in this study, we explored the feasibility of using a publicly available LLM, Vicuna-13B, for the task of identifying findings on reports. Despite Vicuna being a far smaller model than GPT-4, our results largely corroborate the findings by Adams et al ( 2 ) for English-language reports: The performance of Vicuna is comparable with that of CheXpert and CheXbert in terms of AUC, and we observed slight to almost perfect agreement of their outputs on NIH reports when using an adapted version of their prompt (prompt 1). While GPT-4 was able to output valid JSON files in 100% of the cases (170 of 170) ( 2 ), we found that only 23 883 of 25 596 NIH reports (93.3%) and 3014 of 3269 MIMIC reports (92.2%) had valid JSON outputs from Vicuna on task 2. This is likely due to the much larger sample size of our test sets (3269 for MIMIC and 25 596 for NIH vs 170), as well as poorer performance of Vicuna compared with GPT-4. For labeling task 1, Vicuna outputs using prompt 2 agreed better with CheXpert and CheXbert than Vicuna outputs using prompt 1 on both the MIMIC and NIH data sets (with prompt 1, the agreement was poor on average, while it was fair to moderate with prompt 2). Prompt 1 is a simple single-step prompt that, perhaps, fails to capture the semantic differences between the “unsure” and “not mentioned” classes. On the other hand, prompt 2 is a multistep interactive (rule-based) prompt that asks the model a series of binary questions based on the successive model outputs. This likely makes the task easier for the model, and we found that with prompt 2, Vicuna had a higher AUC than with prompt 1 for nine of 11 findings on the 100-report subset with reference-standard radiologist annotations, and that difference was significant for findings of pleural other ( P = .007) and support devices ( P < .001). Another key aspect of LLMs investigated in this study was the temperature hyperparameter that controls the randomness of model outputs. Increasing the temperature led to higher variability and lower agreement among multiple inference runs of Vicuna. We found that Vicuna finding outputs from three runs using the default temperature setting of 0.7 showed less agreement using prompt 2 compared with prompt 1. This is likely because the longer multistep prompting allows more opportunity for randomness to play a role. The increased randomness at a temperature setting of 0.7 also resulted in fewer valid JSON outputs for task 2 with prompt 1 compared with the temperature setting of 0 (79.6% vs 93.3%; P < .001); experiments at additional temperature settings of 0.35 and 1 seem to confirm this trend. With the radiologist annotations as a reference, the majority vote finding following three runs of 100 reports from the NIH data set on Vicuna with the default temperature of 0.7 had a lower AUC than that of the single run with a temperature setting of 0 for all 11 findings when prompt 2 was used (temperature of 0.7 median AUC, 0.65 [IQR, 0.60–0.71] vs temperature of 0 median AUC, 0.84 [IQR, 0.74–0.93]; P = .002), though the difference was not significant for the findings of consolidation ( P = .62) and pleural other ( P = .41). Our study has several limitations. First, it is known that κ statistics are affected by prevalence and may not reflect true agreement for labels for which the prevalence of one class (eg, negative class) is much higher than that of the other class (eg, positive class) ( 27 )—we have not tried to analyze or correct for differing prevalence among the labels in this study. Second, the human-annotated chest radiography reports used as the ground truth for performance analyses was relatively small and included only 100 reports assessed by one reader from one data set. Thus, future studies that include more human-annotated reports from more readers will need to be carried out to ensure reproducibility of the performance results observed in our study. Third, for task 2, we collapsed the “negative,” “not mentioned,” and “unsure” categories into a single “negative” category. This transformation did not decrease the performance of the CheXpert or CheXbert models; however, we did not analyze the case where “unsure” is mapped to the “positive” class—the reported κ values would likely change in that case. Finally, it is possible that better prompts can be engineered such that the performance of Vicuna improves further; however, such prompt engineering requires human effort and time and may negate the key advantage of such LLMs: good out-of-the-box performance without much human effort or time investment. In conclusion, this proof-of-concept study showed that it may be feasible to use publicly available large language models, such as Vicuna-13B, run locally in a privacy-preserving manner, without any additional training or fine-tuning, to label radiology reports and that the performance may be comparable to other commonly used tools, such as CheXpert and CheXbert, particularly when binary labels for each finding are desired. Acknowledgments Acknowledgments We thank Ninet Sinaii, PhD, MPH, of the Biostatistics and Clinical Epidemiology Service at the National Institutes of Health for her insightful guidance on the statistical analysis performed in this study. Additionally, this study used computational resources of the National Institutes of Health high-performance computing Biowulf cluster. Supported by the Intramural Research Program of the National Institutes of Health Clinical Center. Disclosures of conflicts of interest: P.M. No relevant relationships. B.H. No relevant relationships. R.B.L. No relevant relationships. R.M.S. Cooperative research and development agreement with PingAn; patent royalties or software licenses from iCAD, Philips, PingAn, ScanMed, Translation Holdings, and MBG; advisory board member for Duke University. Abbreviations: AUC area under the receiver operating characteristic curve JSON JavaScript object notation LLM large language model NIH National Institutes of Health References 1. Haver HL , Ambinder EB , Bahl M , Oluyemi ET , Jeudy J , Yi PH . Appropriateness of breast cancer prevention and screening recommendations provided 