pplementary_figure_2 560kb ikzf1 hibit mv4 hibit hibit ikzf1 mv4 nihms1994268 supplementary_figure_3 k99gm138758 r00gm138758 ca013330 1s10od016305 scripps cravatt tsaprailis scripps aguilan sidoli jchem chemaxon chemaxon glues chory crabtree eaao5902 burslem proteolysis chimeras kozicka glue glue degraders girona cereblon pomalidomide kronke ikzf1 ikzf3 cereblon ikaros kronke ck1α matyskiela cereblon crl4 crbn ligase petzold ck1α crl4 crbn ligase glue celmod cereblon matyskiela sall4 cereblon sall4 duane matyskiela sall4 pomalidomide cereblon ddb1 sulfonamides rbm39 dcaf15 uehara caperα sulfonamides faust complementarity e7820 rbm39 dcaf15 bussiere indisulam rbm39 dcaf15 ligase słabicki cdk cr8 glue degrader depletes glue degraders nishiguchi ck1α degraders sievers c2h2 degrome crbn matyskiela cereblon crl4 crbn ligase cereblon nishiguchi bioavailable degraders cereblon glue degraders isogenic ligase helios destabilizes bonazzi ikzf2 glue degrader garnar wortzel enl af9 yeats biocompatible sufex thionyl tetrafluoride sof4 bioconjugation angew sufex ishoey heterobifunctional phthalimide degraders jochem degradome degraders glues glue miniaturized bet bromodomain cdk9 e19 cdk6 gygi ms3 isobaric crbn cereblon nihms1994268 supplementary_figure_1 hibit hibit hibit nihms1994268 supplementary_figure_2 560kb ikzf1 hibit mv4 hibit hibit ikzf1 mv4 nihms1994268 supplementary_figure_3','Abstract Molecular glues are small molecules that stabilize protein-protein interactions, enabling new molecular pharmacologies, such as targeted protein degradation. They offer advantages over proteolysis targeting chimeras (PROTACs), which present challenges associated with the size and properties of heterobifunctional constructions, but glues lack the rational design principles analogous to PROTACs. One notable exception is the ability to alter the structure of Cereblon (CRBN)-based molecular glues and redirect their activity toward new neo -substrate proteins. We took a focused approach toward modifying the CRBN ligand, 5’-amino lenalidomide, to alter its neo -substrate specificity using high-throughput chemical diversification by parallelized sulfur(VI)-fluoride exchange (SuFEx) transformations. We synthesized over 3,000 analogs of 5’-amino lenalidomide using this approach and screened the crude products using a phenotypic screen for cell viability, identifying dozens of analogs with differentiated activity. We characterized four compounds that degrade G-to-S phase transition 1 (GSPT1) protein, providing a proof-of-concept model for SuFEx-based discovery of CRBN molecular glues. Keywords: SuFEx, lenalidomide, CRBN, molecular glue, GSPT1, degradation 1. Introduction Drugs that act via chemically induced proximity have demonstrated that it is possible to rewire cellular biochemistry by stabilizing novel protein-protein interactions (PPIs). 1 , 2 Unlike drugs that antagonize an active site or disrupt PPIs, small molecules that stabilize PPIs can create neomorphic functions, often to therapeutic effect. This concept has gained significant traction with E3 ubiquitin ligases, where chemically induced proximity can be harnessed to elicit targeted protein degradation. Proteolysis targeted chimeras (PROTACs) – bifunctional molecules that independently bind to an E3 and a target of interest – feature a modular design, which has allowed for the rational and widespread development of chemical biology tools that induce targeted protein degradation. 3 However, this approach is limited to protein targets that are inherently ligandable by small molecules. In contrast, molecular glues can create or stabilize PPIs without independently binding to both proteins and can therefore target proteins for which small-molecule ligands do not exist. 4 This is perhaps best exemplified by thalidomide and its derivatives, which bind and resurface the E3 substrate receptor, CRBN, such that it stably engages neo -substrates that are then ubiquinated and degraded. 5 - 15 The most common neo -substrate targets of these drugs are C2H2 zinc finger transcription factors that share a β-hairpin structural degron, yielding both their anti-cancer effects (degradation of the B-cell transcription factors IKZF1/3) and their teratogenicity (degradation of the developmental transcription factor SALL4). In general, these transcription factors would be considered classically undruggable targets, which has galvanized interest in molecular glue pharmacology. Notwithstanding the complexities of linker pharmacology, the construction of PROTACs offers a modular design approach wherein ligands for a protein of interest and an E3 ligase can be rationally selected. Molecular glues, conversely, can be used to target otherwise undruggable proteins but lack rational approaches to design them. Most molecular glues have been discovered through retrospective mechanism of action studies. 1 , 2 , 4 Thalidomide analogs were only discovered to function as molecular glues years after their successful repurposing as multiple myeloma therapeutics. Indisulam and related aryl-sulfonamides that degrade the splicing factors RBM23 and RBM39 were similarly annotated post hoc as molecular glues. 16 - 19 Multiple groups have developed successful approaches to identify molecular glue degraders by leveraging their reliance on certain aspects of the ubiquitin-proteasome system. Small-molecule degraders of cyclin K were identified by mining drug sensitivity data across hundreds of cell lines for correlations with mRNA levels of ubiquitin ligase machinery. 20 Phenotypic screening in isogenic cell lines was used to identify neddylation-dependent antiproliferative effects, resulting in the identification of cyclin RBM39 degraders and structurally differentiated cyclin K degraders. 21 , 22 A more focused approach is to modify existing E3 ligase ligands in an attempt to alter their neo -substrate specificity. Many C2H2 proteins can be degraded by thalidomide, lenalidomide, and pomalidomide and the neo-substrate specificity of CRBN can be impacted by structural changes to the portion of the molecule that is exposed to the solvent. 23 For example, GSPT1 degraders were discovered by screening a library of thalidomide analogs and multiple groups have prospectively developed IKZF2 degraders through focused medicinal chemistry campaigns. 24 - 30 Given the likelihood that redirecting the neo-substrate specificity of CRBN-binding small molecules scales with the size of the screening collection, there has been significant interest in developing synthetic approaches to assemble libraries of thalidomide analogs for use in phenotypic screens. 25 - 28 This work extends those efforts, demonstrating the facile synthesis of 3,180 lenalidomide analogs using sulfur(VI) fluoride exchange (SuFEx) transformations coupled to a phenotypic screen for CRBN-dependent anti-proliferative effects. As proof-of-concept, we screened the antiproliferative activity of these C5-modified lenalidomide derivatives against multiple cancer cell lines to identify compounds with redirected neo -substrate specificity. 2. Results We have previously developed a high-throughput medicinal chemistry approach that is based on sulfur(VI) fluoride exchange (SuFEx) “click” chemistry transformations. 31 - 33 This approach relies on high-yielding SuFEx reactions between iminosulfur oxydifluorides and aliphatic amines, which proceed to completion under mild aqueous conditions and are therefore amenable to so-called “direct-to-biology” experimentation. That is, the reaction conditions are sufficiently biocompatible to allow crude reaction products to be screened in biological assays without further purification, even in living cells. We considered that this chemistry could be leveraged to assemble a library of thalidomide analogs, which could then be used to identify compounds with redirected neo -substrate specificity through phenotypic screening. For parallel diversification, we used 5’-amino lenalidomide, a thalidomide analog commonly employed for onward chemistry in PROTACs and molecular glues. We selected 5’-amino lenalidomide over other lenalidomide or other thalidomide analogs as the amine handle projects more clearly into the solvent from this position, potentially better tolerating diverse chemical modifications. SOF 4 gas was used to functionalize 5-amino lenalidomide in one step, affording the iminosulfur oxydifluoride ( Figure 1A , Intermediate 1 ). This was reacted in parallel with a library of 3,180 primary and secondary amines in 384 well plates at 37°C for 24 hours (2 mM scale, 5-fold molar excess of amine, in 10 μL of 1:1 DMSO:PBS), yielding the corresponding sulfamide for primary amines or the sulfuramidimidoyl fluoride (SAF) for secondary amines ( Figure 1A ). Our library consists of approximately 43% primary amines and 57% secondary amines, with 37% existing as free bases and 63% formulated as salts or hydrates. Reactions were deemed complete by monitoring multiple representative wells by UPLC-MS, and plates were stored at −20°C between uses. Figure 1. Cell viability screening cascade of SuFEx diversified lenalidomide analogs. Open in a new tab (A) Synthetic workflow for iminosulfur oxydifluoride functionalized 5’-amino lenalidomide and downstream high-throughput SuFEx click chemistry. Primary and secondary amines (3,180 in total) are reacted with parent ligand in parallel to afford analogs within 24 hours under mild conditions. (B) Screening cascade of 3,180 5’-amino lenalidomide analogs. (C) Viability data approximated by ATP-dependent luminescence (ATPLite) and normalized to DMSO plotted, from left to right, for wild type cell lines (5 μM, 72 h, n = 1) versus free amine molecular weight, wild type (5 μM, 72 h, n = 3) versus CRBN knockout (5 μM, 72 h, n = 3), and wild-type (5 μM, 72 h, n = 3) versus GSPT1 G575N (5 μM, 72 h, n = 3). Colored compounds represent hits at each stage of screening. Compounds outlined in black are shared hits across all three cell lines in the primary screen. Where applicable, data points represent a ratio relative to DMSO average. We elected to screen our library of compounds for anti-proliferative effects against MOLT4 (T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, or T-ALL), MV4;11 (acute myeloid leukemia, AML), and BE(2)-C (neuroblastoma, NB) cells to represent both hematological and solid malignancies. Our screening cascade began by assaying for anti-proliferative activity after incubating compounds with cells for a period of 72 hours at a concentration of 5 μM in a single replicate ( Figure 1B ). The standard deviation of DMSO relative luminescence signal in the primary screen of MOLT4, MV4;11 and BE(2)-C was 0.24, 0.21, and 0.26, respectively. To remain conservative after the singlicate primary screen, we called hits as compounds that elicited a 40% loss in signal for all cell lines. We found that a subset of compounds from our library fit this criterion ( Figure 1C ). 54 compounds were uniquely antiproliferative in the hematological malignancies versus BE(2)-C, and 19 compounds exhibited significant antiproliferative activity across all three cell lines. We noted that a far greater number of hits registered in MOLT4 and MV4;11 cells than in the BE(2)-C. Putative hits from each cell line were retested in triplicate and counter screened in CRBN-deficient matched cell lines to determine if their antiproliferative activity was CRBN-dependent. This was intended to remove compounds with off-target toxicity (due to the compound itself or the excess amine in the crude reaction mixture) and therefore prioritize compounds with CRBN-based on-target activity. CRBN depletion was accomplished by CRISPR/Cas9-mediated gene disruption and validated by immunoblot analysis ( Supplementary Figure 1 ). A subset of our original hits demonstrated CRBN-dependent cytotoxicity, eliciting an average reduction in cell viability of 40% or greater relative to DMSO-treated controls and less than 40% reduction in CRBN-depleted cells ( Figure 1C ). Namely, in MOLT4 cells, 50% of hits reconfirmed when re-tested in triplicate in wild-type cells, with 89% of reconfirmed hits being CRBN-dependent. In MV4;11 cells, 80% of original hits reconfirmed with 80% of those hits being CRBN dependent. In BE(2)-C cells, those percentages were 40% and 50%. Validated hits were next screened for degradation of the G-to-S phase transition 1 (GSPT1) protein, which is commonly degraded by thalidomide analogs and their PROTAC derivatives. 24 - 26 , 28 , 34 Mutation of Gly 575 to Asn renders GSPT1 insensitive to degradation by CRBN-based molecular glues as it disrupts critical contacts with the β-harpin structural degron. 24 We inserted the G575N mutation by CRISPR/Cas9-based gene editing and selected for mutant alleles using CC-885, a potent degrader of GSPT1. 24 Successful editing was confirmed for each cell line population using Sanger sequencing. We then counter-screened all CRBN-dependent hits in triplicate for viability effects in cells harboring the GSPT1 G575N allele ( Figure 1B ). Compounds that appeared to show cell-line-selective activity were tested in their respective cell line, whereas compounds that were active in all cell lines or both hematological cell lines were tested only in MV4;11 cells. If the antiproliferative activity of the compounds were due to the CRBN-dependent degradation of an essential protein other than GSPT1, they would have retained antiproliferative activity in the GSPT1 G575N mutants. However, we saw that the cytotoxic activity of every compound was rescued by GSPT1 G575N , putatively suggesting that all hits were degraders of GSPT1 ( Figure 1C ). We therefore selected 4 compounds for resynthesis that showed dramatic activity in all 3 cell lines and shared structurally similar chemical features ( Figure 2A ). Figure 2. Resynthesized hits elicit CRBN-dependent and GSPT1-depeendent antiproliferative effects in cancer cell lines. Open in a new tab (A) Chemical structures of four hits selected for further characterization (see Methods ). (B) Results of 10-point dose response of 72-hour viability approximated by ATP-dependent luminescence (ATPLite) data in MOLT4 (maroon), MV4;11 (blue) and BE(2)-C (gold) wild type, CRBN KO, and GSPT1 G575N cells, respectively (mean ± SEM, n = 3). Compound 1 possesses a halogen substituted aryl ring appended to the sulfamide through a phenol ether, whereas Compounds 2-4 are substituted with alkyl phenol ethers. All compounds resynthesized were hits from primary amines; no SAFs were represented. Notably, all of these compounds contain ortho-substituted alkyl phenol ether group, highlighting the importance of this moiety for the activity in cells. We tested these compounds for antiproliferative activity in dose-response format, using CC-885 as a positive control. Each compound demonstrated approximately 100-fold more potent antiproliferative activity at three days in the hematological cell lines than in the solid cell line, potentially explaining the greater number of hits in those cell lines ( Figure 2B , Table 1 ). Additionally, we screened these compounds in dose-response format against the CRBN-depleted and GSPT1 G575N mutant cell lines to ensure that the effects of cell viability were rescued by the respective genetic alteration. In both the CRBN depleted cells and the GSPT1 G575N mutants ( Figure 2B ), the antiproliferative effects were rescued in all cell lines. We therefore postulated that the antiproliferative activity of these compounds was due to their ability to degrade the essential protein GSPT1. Table 1. Compound EC 50 in 3-day viability assay and GSPT1 DC 50 in 3-hour degradation assay. Cell Line MOLT4 MV4;11 BE(2)-C EC 50 (μM) EC 50 (μM) EC 50 (μM) Compound 1 0.18 0.19 12 Compound 2 0.06 0.04 13 Compound 3 0.42 0.41 42 Compound 4 0.11 0.08 18 CC-885 <1 nM <1 nM <5 nM DC 50 (μM) DC 50 (μM) DC 50 (μM) Compound 1 0.28 0.28 8 Compound 2 0.09 0.12 14 Compound 3 0.70 0.70 19 Compound 4 0.09 0.06 7 CC-885 <1 nM <1 nM <1 nM Open in a new tab To test if Compounds 1-4 function as GSPT1 degraders, we first subjected all cell lines to treatment with Compounds 1-4 at 3 μM for 3 hours and examined GSPT1 expression through immunoblot analysis. Each compound elicited robust degradation of GSPT1 in the wild-type cell lines, with Compound 3 being the least active ( Figure 3A ). Degradation was rescued by loss of CRBN and by GSPT1 G575N mutation. To characterize the dose-responsive degradation of GSPT1 by Compounds 1-4 , we engineered carboxy-terminal fusions of HiBiT to GSPT1, an 11 amino acid tag that allows for luminescence-based detection of protein abundance through a split NanoLuc complementation system. To do so, we utilized CRISPR/Cas9-based homology directed repair to modify MOLT4, MV4;11 and BE(2)-C cells and validated knock-in by CC-885 treatment ( Supplementary Figure 2 ). We then treated each cell line with Compounds 1-4 , following 1-hour pre-treatment with DMSO or one of three controls. The pomalidomide control (25 μM) served as a direct competition rescue to confirm that degradation occurs through CRBN engagement. MLN4924 (1 μM) and Carfilzomib (500 nM) inhibit NEDD8-dependent activation of Cullin-RING ubiquitin ligase complexes and the proteasome respectively, key components of the CRBN-dependent degradation pathway. The pre-treated cells were then treated for three hours with Compounds 1-4 in dose response and assayed for luminescence signal. Near-complete loss of HiBiT signal was observed in the hematological cell lines at 3 hours at a concentration as low as 1 μM, with robust depletion of signal also observed in the BE(2)-Cs ( Figure 3B , DC 50 values in Table 1 ). Pomalidomide, MLN4924, and Carfilzomib all rescued GSPT1 from degradation by Compounds 1-4 . We observed that these compounds were more potently anti-proliferative in the hematological cell lines when compared to neuroblastoma. The shift in the viability curves matches the shifted degradation curves well, suggesting that any cell-type-selective effects were caused by a lack of degradation of GSPT1 and not a lack of sensitivity to GSPT1 degradation in certain cell lines ( Figure 2B , Figure 3B ). Figure 3. Compounds 1-4 degrade GSPT1. Open in a new tab (A) Immunoblot analysis of GSPT1 degradation in MOLT4, MV4;11, and BE(2)-C wild-type and rescue cell lines after 3 μM treatment with degrader for 3 h. (B) Result of 10-point dose response of degrader treatment (3 μM, 3 h) in MOLT4, MV4;11 and BE(2)-C cells endogenously expressing GSPT1 c-terminal HiBiT fusion, as approximated by HiBiT assay (means ± SEM., n = 3). Prior to treatment with Compounds 1-4 , cells were either pretreated with DMSO, pomalidomide (25 μM, 1 h), MLN4924 (1 μM, 1 h) or Carfilzomib (500 nM, 1 h). (C) 16-plex TMT-labeled expression proteomics analysis of MV4;11 cells incubated with 3 μM compounds for 3 h ( n = 3). Fold change relative to DMSO treated controls ( n = 4). CC-885 is known to degrade IKZF1 and IKZF3 in addition to GSPT1. 35 To evaluate the selectivity of our compounds for degradation of GSPT1, we performed an unbiased expression proteomics analysis in MV4;11 cells. We treated MV4;11s with DMSO or Compounds 1-4 at a concentration of 3 μM for 3 hours. Proteomes were harvested from each sample, multiplexed with 16-plex isobaric tandem mass tag (TMT) labels, bulked, and fractionated on the HPLC before being subjected to mass spectrometry. In each treatment condition, we see that the abundance of GSPT1, along with its close paralog GSPT2, is significantly reduced when compared to the DMSO treated controls ( Figure 3C ). Compounds 1 and 4 showed no degradation of the lenalidomide neo -substrate IKZF1 by expression proteomics. We constructed IKZF1 cHiBiT knock-in cells to test this orthogonally and found that Compound 1 did degrade IKZF1 but Compound 4 did not ( Supplementary Figure 3A ). To ensure that the discrepancy for Compound 1 was not an artifact of the HiBiT reporter tag, we also performed immunoblot analyses of IKZF1 abundance following treatment with Compounds 1-4 ( Supplementary Figure 3B ). These data matched the HiBiT results, altogether confirming that the lack of IKZF1 degradation in the proteomics analysis of Compound 1 was a false-negative result and highlighting the importance of using orthogonal measures of degradation. Altogether, these data confirmed the ability of SuFEx-based high-throughput medicinal chemistry, coupled to phenotypic screening, to identify compounds with redirected neo -substrate specificity, including 1 compound that switched from selective degradation of IKZF1 to selective degradation of GSPT1/2. 3. Discussion and Conclusion Molecular glue degraders are useful chemical biology tools with promising therapeutic potential. The field would benefit from methods to discover new glues prospectively, yet most reports have relied on serendipity. CRBN offers a potential solution, as it can degrade a wide range of proteins with the β-hairpin structural degron in response to thalidomide and its analogs. 5 - 7 , 10 , 14 , 15 Many groups, both in academia and industry, have therefore worked to assemble bespoke small-molecule libraries of thalidomide analogs, reasoning that these analogs could be coupled to biological assays to identify molecular glues that degrade new targets of interest. 23 - 28 We considered that SuFEx-based high-throughput chemistry might be particularly well-suited for this type of effort, since it allows for the rapid synthesis and evaluation of exceptionally large numbers of derivatives. In fact, the size of the library is only limited by the number of amine-functionalize building blocks that are available. In this study, we used a library of over three thousand amines to quickly, easily, and economically, fashion over three thousand molecules for immediate testing in living cells. The SuFEx platform is complementary to other direct-to-biology conditions, such as hydrazide-aldehyde coupling described for the development of CRBN molecular glues. 36 Here, the synthesis of the difluoride functionalized parent ligands is accomplished in a single step, the product is stable and drug-like, and amine coupling partners are more easily accessed and purchased than aryl aldehyde building blocks. As proof of concept, we screened compounds using appropriate counter-screening measures to rapidly identify true-positive hits with the ability to elicit CRBN-dependent antiproliferative effects. Four compounds were resynthesized, characterized, and further evaluated, ultimately being identified as degraders of GSPT1/2. Similar efforts to synthesize large collections of thalidomide analogs, including other direct-to-biology approaches, have similarly arrived at degraders of GSPT1, 25 , 26 , 28 which is pan-essential and highly degradable. Screens that are based on cell viability, survival, or proliferation are therefore highly likely to identify GSPT1 degraders. We found this to be a benefit in this study, as it allowed us to rapidly identify the target of compounds with redirected neo -substrate specificity. However, now that we have established proof-of-concept for this SuFEx-based approach, we envision that future efforts will benefit from phenotypic screening assays that would not be affected by GSPT1 degradation. A recent study from the Winter group, which used the cell painting assay for a morphological screen to identify a GSPT2 degrader that spares GSPT1, provides a useful example for future efforts. 27 4. Methods 4.1.1. Cell Culture MOLT4 and MV4;11 WT, CRBN knockout, and GSPT1-G575N mutants were cultured in RPMI-1640 containing L-glutamine supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum (FBS) and 1x anti-biotic/anti-mycotic (Gibco, cat. Number 15240096). BE(2)-C WT, CRBN knockout, and GSPT1-G575N mutants were cultured in DMEM containing glucose, L-glutamine and sodium pyruvate, supplemented with 10% FBS and 1x anti-biotic/anti-mycotic. Mycoplasma testing (Lonza MycoAlert Detection Kit) was conducted monthly throughout the duration of experiments and all cells remained negative. 4.1.2. SuFEx Library Synthesis A library of 3,180 primary and secondary amines was purchased from ChemBridge. Using the Beckman Coulter Labcyte Echo, 2 μL of amine (50 mM stock, 5 eq, 10 mM final) was transferred into 384 well low dead volume plates (Catalog # LP-0200). Subsequently, 3 μL of (2-(2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl)-1-oxoisoindolin-5-yl)sulfurimidoyl difluoride (6.67 mM stock, 1 eq, 2 mM final) was transferred to each well of the plates via matrix pipette. 5 μL of PBS (pH = 8, freshly prepared) was added to each well via matrix pipette for a total reaction volume of 10 μL. Plates were covered with foil and placed in a 37°C oven for 24 hours. UPLC analysis of random wells representing different coupling partners (primary amines, secondary amines, mono- and bis-HCl salts) indicated reaction completion. Plates were covered with foil adhesive and stored at −20°C between uses. Plates were allowed to thaw at room temperature and spun at 200 RCF for 5 minutes prior to use. 4.1.3. Primary, Counter- and Re-Screening Derivatized lenalidomide molecules were stamped onto 384 well plates (Greiner, white polystyrene) at a volume of 100 nL (0.125% DMSO). Suspension cells were plated in a volume of 40 μL at a density of 5,000 cells per well and adherent cells were plated at a density of 2,500 cells per well using a Multidrop Combi Automated Reagent Dispenser. Cells were allowed to incubate with compound (5 μM final concentration) for 72 hours before cell viability was assayed using ATP Lite. ATP Lite reagent was prepared fresh by resuspending lyophilized substrate in 10 mL of resuspension buffer and diluting with 35 mL of H 2 O. 20 μL of ATP Lite reagent was added to each well using the Multidrop Combi. Plates were covered with foil and placed on a shaker for 30 minutes at room temperature. Luminescence was read using an Envision plate reader, and viability was normalized to DMSO treated controls. 4.1.4. CRBN KO MV4;11 and MOLT4 cells deficient in CRBN were a gift from the lab of Prof. Georg Winter. 37 , 38 BE(2)-C cells functionally deficient in CRBN were generated through CRISPR-Cas9 mediated genetic knockout. gRNA complexes were formed by incubating 1:1 volume of tracrRNA (IDT, 160 μM) and crRNA (IDT, 160 μM, 5’-ACCAATGTTCATATAAATGG-3’) at 37°C for 30 minutes in a thermocycler. To the resulting 80 μM gRNA solution was added 2.7 μL Cas9 Nuclease v3 (IDT, 60 μM) and 1.3 μL of duplex buffer (IDT). The components were incubated at 37°C for 15 minutes in the thermocycler to pre-form the RNP. 1 μL of RNP solution (20 pmol) was electroporated into 200,000 cells that were washed and resuspended in electroporation buffer. Cells were moved into antibiotic free medium and allowed to recover. CRBN KO was confirmed through immunoblotting, as well as through treatment with DBET6. 4.1.5. GSPT1 G575N Mutants in MOLT4, MV4;11, BE(2)-C Cells containing a glycine to asparagine mutation at position 575 were generated in all three cell lines through using CRISPR-Cas9. gRNA complexes were formed by incubating 1:1 volume of tracrRNA (IDT, 160 μM) and crRNA (IDT, 160 μM, 5’-TGCTTGGTAGACAAAAAATC-3’) at 37°C for 30 minutes in a thermocycler. To the resulting 80 μM gRNA solution was added 2.7 μL Cas9 Nuclease v3 (IDT, 60 μM) and 1.3 μL of duplex buffer (IDT). The components were incubated at 37°C for 15 minutes in the thermocycler to pre-form the RNP. 1 μL of RNP solution (20 pmol) alongside 1 μL of ssODN (IDT, 2 μM, 5’- cttttcttggttaggccttaatctgcttggtagacaaaaaatcaaacgaaaaaagtaagacccgaccccgttttgtcaaacaagatc-3’) was electroporated into 200,000 cells that were washed and resuspended in electroporation buffer. Cells were moved into antibiotic free medium and allowed to recover. GSPT1-G575N cells were selected in 1 μM CC-885 and kept in selection for duration of experiments. G575N mutation was confirmed in each cell line via Sanger sequencing (Genewiz; Sequencing primer 5’- agttgaccagggacttgccaagaag-3’). 4.1.6. GSPT1-cHiBiT Mutants in MOLT4, MV4;11, BE(2)-C gRNA complexes were formed by incubating 1:1 volume of tracrRNA (IDT, 160 μM) and crRNA (IDT, 160 μM, 5’- acacacggcccccccgatca-3’) at 37°C for 30 minutes in a thermocycler. To the resulting 80 μM gRNA solution was added 2.7 μL Cas9 Nuclease v3 (IDT, 60 μM) and 1.3 μL of duplex buffer (IDT). The components were incubated at 37°C for 15 minutes in the thermocycler to pre-form the RNP. 1 μL of RNP solution (20 pmol) alongside 1 μL of ssODN (IDT, 2 μM, 5’- aagaccattgcaattggaaaagttctgaaactggttccagagaaagacggaagcggaatggTAAGCGGCTGGCGGCTGTTCAAGAAGATTAGCtaaacaatactgtgaggaaaattgactgcagaagcctacttcacaccgccttc-3’) was electroporated into 200,000 cells that were washed and resuspended in electroporation buffer. Cells were moved into antibiotic free medium and allowed to recover. Cells were assayed for HiBiT signal, and those demonstrating robust signal over parental WT were expanded. 4.1.7. IKZF1-cHiBiT Mutants in MV4;11 gRNA complexes were formed by incubating 1:1 volume of tracrRNA (IDT, 160 μM) and crRNA (IDT, 160 μM, 5’-CTCAGATAACCTGAGGACCA-3’) at 37°C for 30 minutes in a thermocycler. To the resulting 80 μM gRNA solution was added 2.7 μL Cas9 Nuclease v3 (IDT, 60 μM) and 1.3 μL of duplex buffer (IDT). The components were incubated at 37°C for 15 minutes in the thermocycler to pre-form the RNP. 1 μL of RNP solution (20 pmol) alongside 1 μL of ssODN (IDT, 2 μM, 5’- ttttgctttaaactaaaatcccttcctctctttctcagataacctgaggaatggTAAGCGGCTGGCGGCTGTTCAAGAAGATTAGCggaagcggaatggatgctgatgagggtcaagacatgtcccaagtttcaggtgagacctt-3’) was electroporated into 200,000 cells that were washed and resuspended in electroporation buffer. Cells were moved into antibiotic free medium and allowed to recover. Cells were assayed for HiBiT signal, and those demonstrating robust signal over parental WT were expanded. 4.1.8. HiBiT Detection Assay Detection reagent was made up according to assay protocols (Nano-Glo HiBiT Lytic Detection System, Cat. No. N3030). Cells were plated at 20,000 cells per well in 20 uL of media. 20 uL of detection reagent was added to each well and allowed to incubate at room temperature in the dark for 15 minutes before luminescence signal was read. 4.1.9. Dose Response A compound plate was made up in an Echo Qualified 384 well plate with a 10 mM top concentration and 3-fold serial dilution. Compounds were stamped onto assay plates in triplicate, after which cells were added to plates and allowed to incubate before being assayed for cell viability or HiBiT signal. Cells were plated at 5,000 or 2,500 cells per well for suspension and adherent cells respectively in 40 μL of media for viability assays and 20 μL for HiBiT assays. 4.1.10. Immunoblotting 25 μg of prepared protein sample was added to each well of a Bolt 12% Bis-Tris Plus gel (Invitrogen) and run at 90V for 10 minutes followed by 150V for 45 minutes. The gel was transferred to a nitrocellulose membrane at 10 V for 70 minutes. Primary antibodies were purchased from Cell Signaling Technologies (anti-CRBN; D8H3S), Novus (anti-GSPT1; NBP2-16754) and Santa Cruz Biotechnologies (anti-GAPDH; 6C5). Primary antibodies were diluted 1:1000, 1:1000 and 1:5000, respectively. Membranes were blocked with 5% milk in TBS-T, after which membranes were incubated with primary antibodies at 4°C overnight with shaking. The appropriate secondary antibodies were purchased from LiCor (IRDye680 goat-anti-mouse 925-68070; IRDye800 goat-anti-rabbit 926-32211) and diluted at 1:5000. Secondary antibodies were incubated on membranes for 1 hour with shaking at room temperature before being washed in TBST and imaged on the LiCor. 4.1.11. Expression Proteomics MV4;11 cells were treated as following: 10e6 cells per condition were resuspended in 10 mL of complete media in a T25 flask. Cells were treated with either DMSO (four replicates) or Compound 1-4 (3 replicates per compound) at a concentration of 3 μM for 3 hours. Cells were harvested by centrifugation at 4°C for 5 minutes at 500 × g. Cells were washed twice with ice cold PBS and transferred into 1.5 mL Eppendorf tubes, after which they were pelleted at 1000 × g for 5 minutes. The PBS was aspirated, and the pellets were frozen in LN2 and stored at −80°C until they were ready for use. Cells were thawed on ice. Lysis buffer was prepared by placing 1 tablet of Roche Complete ULTRA mini EDTA-free Protease Inhibitor Cocktail in 10 mL of DPBS. 200 μL of lysis buffer was added to each sample, and cells were lysed by probe sonication using a Branson Sonifer 250 ultrasonic cell disrupter (2 × 15 pulses, 10% power output, allowing Eppendorf to rest on ice between first and second round.) The protein concentration was determined by DC assay. Samples were diluted 1:2 in DPBS+PIC, and 2 μL of diluted sample was added to each well in triplicate. A standard curve was made in duplicate using 1 μL, 2 μL, 3 μL, 4 μL and 5 μL of a 2 mg/mL solution of BSA. 20 μL of Reagent A was added to each well using a multichannel pipette with mixing. 160 μL of Reagent B was added to each well using a multichannel pipette with mixing. The plate was incubated for 10 minutes and then read at Absorbance 750 nm. The protein concentration was calculated from the standard curve. 48 mg of urea was measured into a lo-bind safe lock 1.5 mL Eppendorf tube per sample. 200 ug proteome for each condition was added and brought to a total of 64 μL DPBS+PIC [8 M urea]. Tubes were flicked to ensure that urea is fully dissolved and the sample was clear. A fresh stock of DTT (31 mg/mL in MilliQ H 2 O) was prepared. 5 μL was added to each sample [10 mM DTT] and incubated at 65°C for 15 minutes, flicking the tubes halfway through and allowing them to cool to room temperature upon completion. A fresh stock of iodoacetamide (IA, 74 mg/mL in MilliQ H 2 O) was prepared. 5 μL was added to each sample [20 mM IA] and incubated at 37°C for 30 minutes with shaking. 500 μL of ice-cold MeOH, 100 μL of ice-cold CHCl3 and 400 μL of ice-cold H 2 O was added to each sample to remove urea and clean up each sample. Samples were spun at 16,000 × g for 10 minutes, allowing a protein disc to form between the organic and aqueous layers. A gel loading tip and vacuum aspirator was used to remove the liquid, being careful not to disturb the protein disc. 1 mL of ice cold MeOH was added to each tube, and the samples were spun again for 10 minutes at 16,000 × g before removing the supernatant. The eppies were left uncapped to allow the MeOH to dry out. 160 μL of 200 mM EPPS, pH 8.0 was added to each sample [protein concentration: ~1.25 mg/mL]. The pellet was sonicated into solution (10% output, 8×1 pulses, 1 second on, 1 second off). 1 μL of 2 mg/mL LysC solution was added and incubated at 37°C with shaking for one hour. While shaking, trypsin solution was prepared. To each vial of trypsin was added 48 μL of trypsin buffer and 12 μL of 100 mM CaCl 2 solution. 7 μL of trypsin (2.64 ug) was added to each sample and the proteins were digested overnight at 37°C with shaking. Peptide concentration was determined using Thermos Micro BCA assay as per kit protocol. 25 ug of peptide was added into a clean, lo-bind Eppendorf tube and brought to a total of 40 μL with 200 mM EPPS. 10 μL of LCMS grade ACN was added to each sample. 6 uL (20 μg/μL in dry ACN) of TMT 16plex tag solution was added to the appropriate samples, and were incubated at room temperature for an hour, vortexing twice during incubation. Labeling was quenched by adding 6.0 μL of 5% hydroxylamine solution (prepared by diluting 50% hydroxylamine 1:10 in HPLC grade H 2 O). The reaction was neutralized with 3.1 μL of formic acid (FA, 5% final) and the samples were vacuum-centrifuged to near dryness. Approximately 300 μL of Buffer A (95:5:0.1 H 2 O:ACN:FA) was added to the first sample, resuspending the pellet. The sample was vortexed briefly and spun down. Using the same lo-bind pipette tip, the 300 μL from tube 1 was transferred to tube 2, repeating the process. This was repeated through all 16 samples. 200 μL of fresh Buffer A was added to tube 1, and the process was repeated. All tubes were spun down again, and a lo-bind P20 tip was used to transfer all remaining liquid to the final tube. Ultimately, all 16 samples were bulked into 500 μL of Buffer A. The sample was vacuum-centrifuged until dry and stored at −80 until ready for fractionation. The sample was resuspended in 500 μL Buffer A (95:5:0.1 H 2 O:ACN:FA). An additional 30 μL of formic acid was added to ensure sample acidity. The sample was sonicated in water bath for five minutes. The desalting cartridge was condition with 3 × 1 mL of 100% ACN, followed by an equilibration with 3 × 1 mL Buffer A, ensuring the column never ran dry. The sample was loaded onto the column slowly into a clean Eppendorf tube at a rate of 1 drop per second. The flow through was reloaded onto the column. The sample was desalted by passing 3 × 1 mL of Buffer A. The sample was eluted into a clean Eppendorf tube using 1 mL of Buffer B (80:20:0.1 ACN:H 2 O:FA). The cartridge was blown entirely dry ensuring not to spray the sample. The sample was vacuum-centrifuged overnight. The sample was resuspended in 500 μL of Buffer A and placed in a water bath to sonicate for 5 minutes before spinning down at top speed in a tabletop centrifuge for 1 minute. The redissolved sample was fractionated on an Agilent HPLC (Agilent Infinity 1260 II system) into a 96 deep-well plate containing 20 μL of 20% FA. Prior to injection onto the instrument, the HPLC column was washed in 80% Buffer B and then equilibrated in 100% Buffer A. After washing the loading syringe and loop, the sample was loaded into the loop and subsequently onto the HPLC. The peptides were eluted onto a capillary column (ZORBAX Extend-C18, 80Å, 4.6 × 250 mm, 5 μM) and separated at a flow rate of 0.5 mL/min using the following gradient with buffer A (10 mM aqueous NH 4 HCO 3 ) and buffer B (100% Acetonitrile): 100% buffer A from 0–2 min, 0–13% buffer B from 2–3 min, 13–42% buffer B from 3–60 min, 42–100% buffer B from 60–61 min, 100% buffer B from 61–65 min, 100–0% buffer b from 65–66 min, 100% buffer A from 66–75 min, 0–13% buffer B from 75–78 min, 13–80% buffer B from 78–80 min, 80% buffer B from 80–85 min, 100% buffer A from 86–91 min, 0–13% buffer B from 91–94 min, 13–80% buffer B from 94–96 min, 80% buffer B from 96–101 min, and 80–0% buffer B from 101–102 min. The eluent was evaporated to dryness in the 96 deep-well plate using a SpeedVac vacuum concentrator. The peptides were resuspended in Buffer B and combined into 12 fractions by adding Buffer B to the first row, resuspending, and then transferring solution to the second row, resuspending, and repeating the process down the plate bulking each column into a separate single sample. These 12 samples were dried on the SpeedVac overnight and submitted to mass spec analysis. Dried fractions were solubilized in 50mL of 0.1% trifluoroacetic acid and desalted using 2μg capacity ZipTips (Millipore, Billeric, MA) according to manufacturer instructions. Peptides were then on-line eluted into a Fusion Tribrid mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific, San Jose, CA) from an EASY PepMapTM RSLC C18 column (2μm, 100Å, 75μm × 50cm, Thermo Scientific, San Jose, CA), using a hold of 6% solvent B (80/20 acetonitrile/water, 0.1% formic acid) for 15 minutes, followed by a gradient of 6-28% solvent B in 105 minutes, then 28-49% solvent B in 15 minutes, 40-100% solvent B in 10 minutes, a 10 minute hold of 100% solvent B, a return to 5% solvent B in 3 minutes, and finally a 3 minute hold of 5% solvent B. The gradient was then extended for the purpose of cleaning the column by increasing solvent B to 100% in 3 min, a 100% solvent B hold for 10 min, a return to 5% solvent B in 3 min, a 5% solvent B fold for 3 min, an increase of solvent B again to 100% in 3 min, a 100% solvent B hold for 10min, a return to 5% solvent B in 3 minutes and a 5% solvent B hold for 3 min and finally one last increase to 100% solvent B in 3 minutes and a hold of 100% solvent B for 10min. All flow rates were 250nL/minute delivered using an nEasy-LC1000 nano liquid chromatography system (Thermo Scientific, San Jose, CA). Solvent A consisted of water and 0.1% formic acid. Ions were created at 2.2kV using an EASY Spray source (Thermo Scientific, San Jose, CA) held at 50°C. A synchronous precursor selection (SPS)-MS3 mass spectrometry method was selected based on the work of Ting et al. 39 Scans were conducted between 380-2000 m/z at a resolution of 120,000 for MS1 in the Orbitrap mass analyzer at a standard AGC target (as defined in Thermo Scientific Xcalibur v 4.6.67.17) and a maximum injection of 50 msec. Collision induced dissociation (CID) was then performed in the linear ion trap of peptide monoisotopic ions with charge 2-8 above an intensity threshold of 5E3, using a quadrupole isolation of 0.7 m/z and a CID energy of 35%. The ion trap AGC target was also set to standard with a maximum injection time of 50 msec. Dynamic exclusion duration was set at 60 seconds and ions were excluded after one time within the +/− 10ppm mass tolerance window. The top 10 MS2 ions in the ion trap between 400-1200 m/z were then chosen for Higher-energy C-trap dissociation (HCD) at 65% energy. Detection occurred in the Orbitrap at a resolution of 50,000 and an AGC target of 1E5 (or 200% custom setting as defined in Thermo Scientific Xcalibur v 4.6.67.17), and an injection time of 120msec (MS3). All scan events occurred within a 3-second specified cycle time. The analysis was performed at The Herbert Wertheim UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation & Technology, Mass Spectrometry and Proteomics Core Facility (RRID:SCR_023576). The raw spectra files were uploaded onto the Integrated Proteomics Pipeline (IP2) available at ( http://ip2.scripps.edu/ip2/mainMenu.html ). The MS2 and MS3 files were extracted from the raw files using RAW converter (version 1.1.0.22) and searched using the ProLuCID algorithm against a reverse-concatenated, non-redundant variant of the Human UniProt database (2016-07). Cysteine residues were searched with a static modification for carbamidomethylation (+57.02146 Da) and up to two differential modifications were allowed per peptide for methionine oxidation (+15.994915 Da). N-termini and lysine residues were searched with a static modification for the TMT tag (+304.2071). Peptides were required to be a minimum of 6 amino acid residues. ProLuCID data was filtered using DTAselect 2.0 within IP2 to achieve a peptide false-positive rate below 1%. For the MS3-based quantification, the reporter ion mass tolerance was set to 20 ppm. Additionally, proteins were required to have at least 2 unique peptides. 4.2.1. Synthesis Solvents and chemicals were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO, USA), ChemBridge (San Diego, CA, USA) and Broadpharm (San Diego, CA, USA). Reactions were carried out in a nitrogen atmosphere. Progress of reactions was monitored by Waters I-Class UPLC with diode array and QDa mass spec. 1 H and 13 C NMR spectra were recorded using a Bruker AVIII HD 600 NMR spectrometer equipped with either a 5 mm CPQCI (H-C-N-P) CryoProbe or a 5 mm CPDCH (C-H) CryoProbe, respectively. HRMS data was collected using an Agilent 6239 TOF LC/MS System. HPLC-UV chromatography was performed with an Agilent LC-MSD system with 1290MCT column oven, 1290 Multisampler, 1290 High Speed Pump, 1290 DAD FS, LC/MSD with reverse phase HPLC column Agilent InfinityLab Poroshell 120 EC-C18 (2.1 × 50 mm, particle size 1.9 μM). Absorption monitored at 254 nm. Milli-Q water with 0.1% formic acid (FA) as aqueous phase and acetonitrile with 0.1% FA as organic phase. Flow at 0.50 mL/min. Started at 95% aqueous and 5% organic phases followed by a 4.0 minute gradient to 95% organic phase. Then 30 seconds of 100% organic phase was followed by 30 seconds gradient to 95% aqueous. Prep HPLC was performed with Waters2545 Binary Gradient Module, Waters515 HPLC Pump, Waters SFO System Fluidics organizer, Waters2489 UV/Visible Detector, Waters2424 ELS Detector, Waters QDa Detector, and Waters3767 Sample Manager. A C18 reverse phase column was employed with the following characteristics: Waters, waters X Bridge BEH C18 OBD Prep Column, 19 × 250 mm, 5 μm. Mobile phase as described for analytical HPLC. 4.2.2. Intermediate 1. (2-(2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl)-1-oxoisoindolin-5-yl)sulfurimidoyl difluoride In a 10 mL round-bottom flask, 3-(5-amino-1-oxoisoindolin-2-yl)piperidine-2,6-dione (92.4 mg, 0.36 mmol, Broadpharm) was dissolved in anhydrous DMF (2.4 mL) and N , N -diisopropylethylamine (186 μL, 1.1 mmol, 3.0 Eq) was added. The flask was sealed with a rubber septum, purged with Ar and backfilled with thionyl tetrafluoride gas (~10 mL). The reaction was stirred at room temperature until complete conversion of the starting material verified by LC-MS analysis. The reaction mixture was purified on preparative HPLC to afford the title compound (77.0 mg, 61%). 1 H NMR (600 MHz, DMSO-d 6 ) δ 11.00 (s, 1H), 7.80 (d, J = 8.1 Hz, 1H), 7.55 (d, J = 1.8 Hz, 1H), 7.40 (dd, J = 8.1, 1.8 Hz, 1H), 5.12 (dd, J = 13.3, 5.1 Hz, 1H), 4.49 (d, J = 17.6 Hz, 1H), 4.37 (d, J = 17.6 Hz, 1H), 2.92 (ddd, J = 17.7, 13.7, 5.3 Hz, 1H), 2.64 – 2.58 (brd, J = 16.5 Hz, 1H), 2.44 – 2.36 (m, 1H), 2.04 – 1.99 (m, 1H). 13 C NMR (151 MHz, DMSO-d 6 ) δ 173.3, 171.4, 167.5, 144.6, 138.6, 130.3, 125.2, 124.2, 119.4, 52.2, 47.6, 31.7, 22.9. 19 F NMR (565 MHz, DMSO-d 6 , CFCl 3 ) δ −76.61. HRMS analysis: expected 344.0511, found 344.0519 [M+H] + 4.2.3. Compound 1. (2-(2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl)-1-oxoisoindolin-5-yl)sulfurimidoyl difluoride (1 eq, 35 μMol, 12 mg) was resuspended in a 1:1 mixture of acetonitrile and PBS (pH = 8) and added to a 1 dram vial. 2-(2-chloro-6-methylphenoxy)ethan-1-amine hydrochloride (4 eq, 142.44 umol, 31.638 mg) and DIPEA (4 eq, 142.44 umol, 24.8 μL) were added to the vessel. The reaction was stirred at 37°C overnight, and reaction completion was confirmed by UPLC. The crude reaction was loaded directly onto the column (RediSep Gold, C18, 5.5 g) and purified via reverse phase column chromatography (20-100% ACN in H 2 O) and lyophilized to yield title compound as a white solid (5 mg, 30%). 1 H NMR (600 MHz, DMSO) δ 10.97 (s, 1H), 10.28 (s, 1H), 8.01 (t, J = 6.0 Hz, 1H), 7.63 (d, J = 8.3 Hz, 1H), 7.38 (d, J = 1.9 Hz, 1H), 7.25 (dt, J = 8.3, 2.2 Hz, 2H), 7.15 (d, J = 1.2 Hz, 1H), 7.02 (t, J = 7.8 Hz, 1H), 5.08 (dd, J = 13.3, 5.1 Hz, 1H), 4.32 (dd, 2H), 3.85 (t, J = 6.1 Hz, 2H), 3.24 (q, J = 6.1 Hz, 2H), 2.95 – 2.86 (m, 1H), 2.62 – 2.57 (m, 1H), 2.42 – 2.34 (m, 1H), 2.17 (s, 3H), 2.02 – 1.95 (m, 1H). 13 C NMR (151 MHz, DMSO) δ 172.4, 170.6, 167.3, 152.0, 143.0, 141.7, 132.6, 129.4, 127.3, 126.1, 125.0, 124.6, 123.4, 117.0, 111.1, 70.4, 51.0, 46.5, 41.6, 30.7, 22.0, 15.4 HRMS analysis: expected 507.1100, found 507.1107 [M+H] + 4.2.4. Compound 2. (2-(2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl)-1-oxoisoindolin-5-yl)sulfurimidoyl difluoride (1 eq, 35 μMol, 12 mg) was resuspended in a 1:1 mixture of acetonitrile and PBS (pH = 8) and added to a 1 dram vial. 2-(2-(isopentyloxy)phenyl)ethan-1-amine hydrochloride (4 eq, 142.44 umol, 34.724 mg) and DIPEA (4 eq, 142.44 μMol, 24.8 μL) were added to the vessel. The reaction was stirred at 37°C overnight, and reaction completion was confirmed by UPLC. The crude reaction was loaded directly onto the column (RediSep Gold, C18, 5.5 g) and purified via reverse phase column chromatography (20-100% ACN in H 2 O) and lyophilized to yield title compound as a white solid (2.6 mg, 14%). 1 H NMR (600 MHz, DMSO) δ 10.97 (s, 1H), 10.16 (s, 1H), 7.73 (t, J = 5.7 Hz, 1H), 7.59 (d, J = 8.2 Hz, 1H), 7.29 (s, 1H), 7.19 (dd, J = 8.3, 1.9 Hz, 1H), 7.12 (td, J = 7.4, 1.8 Hz, 1H), 7.01 (dd, J = 7.4, 1.7 Hz, 1H), 6.85 (dd, J = 8.2, 1.1 Hz, 1H), 6.78 (td, J = 7.4, 1.0 Hz, 1H), 5.08 (dd, J = 13.3, 5.1 Hz, 1H), 4.28 (dd, 2H), 3.88 (t, J = 6.6 Hz, 2H), 3.0