cores decreased 2.9 and 3.9 points among those randomized to placebo and trazodone, respectively; the difference in mean change was not significant (p = .145). As a measure of substantive effect size independent of the scale on which parameters were measured we also calculated Cohen’s standardized effect, d. Without adjusting p-values for multiple comparisons, only 1 of 23 comparisons reported in Table 3 was statistically significant at the .05 level. As reported on the morning survey, subjective total sleep time increased by about 45.5 minutes among those receiving trazodone compared to 9.8 minutes in the placebo group (t = −1.99, p = .05); the standardized effect size was .373. Table 3. Mean and Change in Mean Objective and Subjective Sleep Parameters, Baseline to Follow-UP, by Intervention Group. PLACEBO (n = 67) TRAZODONE (n = 67) Baseline Posttest Change Baseline Posttest Change p = a d b Global PSQI Score Baseline to 1 Month 12.8 9.8 −2.9 13.0 9.1 −3.9 .15 .25 Baseline to 3 Months c 12.8 9.2 −3.6 13.1 8.1 −5.0 .07 .32 Baseline to 6 Months d 12.6 9.2 −3.5 12.9 8.4 −4.5 .16 .26 PSG Parameters (n = 56) (n = 63) Sleep Period Time (Minutes) 434.0 400.4 −33.6 441.6 414.1 −27.5 .80 .05 Total Sleep Time (Minutes) 337.6 344.1 6.6 360.1 355.9 −4.2 .62 .09 Sleep Efficiency (%) 82.7 85.3 2.6 83.4 85.8 2.32 .90 .02 Stage 1 Sleep (%) 2.3 2.5 0.2 2.4 2.4 0.0 .67 .08 Stage 2 Sleeps (%) 66.7 63.3 −3.4 66.9 63.3 −3.7 .90 .02 Slow Wave Sleep (%) 13.6 14.5 0.9 12.2 14.5 2.3 .46 .14 REM Sleep (%) 17.4 17.9 0.5 18.5 19.9 1.4 .66 .08 Time Awake (%) 17.3 14.7 −2.6 16.6 14.2 −2.3 .90 .02 Arousal Index 9.8 9.5 −0.3 9.5 8.8 −0.7 .26 .21 Apnea Index 3.7 2.0 −1.8 2.2 2.8 0.6 .77 .05 Morning Survey (n = 55) (n = 60) Min. Sleep Period Time a a 454.9 443.2 −11.7 436.6 449.1 12.5 .23 .23 Min. Total Sleep time 339.6 349.4 9.82 310.0 356.7 45.5 .05 .37 Sleep Efficiency a 74.9 79.7 4.8 71.9 80.1 8.2 .33 .19 Times Awakened 3.1 2.6 −0.5 3.3 2.6 −0.7 .32 .19 Sleep Rating 3.4 3.2 −0.2 3.1 3.2 0.1 .1